hich comments we should pay attention to," says foreign bank trader. Government denies local report that it would announce further measures in early December aimed at curbing speculative foreign capital flows. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0417 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex +0.6% at 19,97.50 in early trade, helped by overnight gains in U.S. markets (DJIA ended +1.2%), short-covering in some bluechips. Market breadth positive with 26 of 30 index issues higher; TCS (532540.BY) +1.5% at INR977.50 before 2Q results later today. "The start is on expected lines. With the index - down over 800 points in previous 5 sessions some buying is happening in select heavyweights," says local dealer. Adds, short-covering helping metal plays. Tips resistance at 20,080, support at 19,850. \uc2 Tata Steel\uc2  (500470.BY) +1.4% at INR624.25, \uc2 Tata Motors\uc2  (500570.BY) +1.4% at INR1,172. \uc2 Reliance Industries\uc2  (500325.BY) +1.2% at INR1,061.50, Sterlite (500900.BY) +1.2% at INR172.30. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0408 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD weaker following U.S. Treasury Secretary Geithner's comment in WSJ interview, China data; pair likely to consolidate in 0.7400-0.7535 range ahead of G-20 this weekend, says \uc2 HIFX\uc2  director of trading Mike Hollows; "The market got unsettled when Geithner made some comments about the value of the U.S. dollar against the euro, the yen and other." Geithner told WSJ that he believes major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are "roughly in alignment now." Says China data did not have much impact "as it was there or there about" expectations. Adds, little top tier data due out overnight, market will be keeping eye on protests in Europe about austerity measure but main focus now on G-20 meeting. Pair last at 0.7510 vs 0.7544 early this morning. Tips support at 0.7490 with resistance at 0.7535. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0406 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY extends losses midday, tracking weaker USD vs Asians. Pair last at 6.6482 vs 6.6514 earlier, 6.6519 last close; 6.6450-6.6500 band tipped for rest of session. "Stronger yuan demand at the end of the month should support the dollar-yuan pair next week," says Shanghai-based US bank trader; exporters routinely sell USD at month's end to banks on request of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Adds 3Q GDP, CPI roughly within expectations, having little impact on market. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0404 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR lower, tracking overnight USD slip, but finding support from importers, says trader with private bank; pair at 44.31 vs 44.35 late Wednesday. Adds, firm local stocks may weigh more, but downward pressure may be offset by importers looking to cover USD needs for import payments before expected repatriation of IPO-directed flows boost pair from current levels. Tips, 44.20-44.25 zone to provide firm support, but notes some long USD positions still need cover, making test of 44.20 likely within session. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0359 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds open lower, but may find support as traders may have overreacted in rush to dump benchmark note on view it may drift into illiquidity if new 10-year note sold in January, says trader with primary dealership; 7.80% 2020 government bond at 97.70 vs 97.75 last close. "Even if you add in a pretty heavy illiquidity premium to the yield, there's still an argument for buying the current benchmark at these levels," says trader. Notes, 10-year, 12-year spreads now inverted, indicating market already more than pricing in illiquidity in 10-year note. Directionally though, market also likely to watch weekly primary price data (due later in session) very carefully for signs that inflation may remain far from ebbing, fresh uptick may stoke talk of further RBI rate hikes beginning from next policy meeting on November 2. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0347 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 near flat at 4624.0, sets intraday low of 4622.0 from early high of 4661.4 as offshore markets lose steam. Shanghai Composite down about 1.0% after China economic data. AUD/USD down 0.4%, pointing negatively for risk assets. Traders attribute most of retreat to disappointment that China's GDP, industrial production data didn't "shoot the lights out" since hopes were high after China unexpectedly raised rates. "China data wasn't as strong as the bulls were hoping and we have just rolled over with the rest of Asia," says senior trader at \uc2 UBS\uc2 . "There was obviously some expectation yesterday that China's growth would be very strong." Adds that long-only funds aren't selling out, yet there's "plenty of nervousness around the banks in particular. It's more the fact there's a fight for deposits, so margins are under pressure." Major banks down 0.4%-1.2%. Resources remain positive, though they set intraday lows after the data. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0347 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ shares +0.5% at 3259.17 after market "got a boost on the back of positive movements in markets overnight," says \uc2 Craigs Investment Partners\uc2  broker Belinda Stanley. Fisher & Paykel Healthcare (FPH.NZ) +1.9% at NZ$3.25, may be benefiting from weaker NZD/USD, Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ) +0.7% at NZ$8.19 after positive migration data earlier in day. Index weighed by Telecom (TEL.NZ) down 0.5% at NZ$2.02. Says recent selling in Telecom may be international investors taking advantage of high NZD/USD to repatriate some funds. Notes lack of local drivers means investors will remain focused on offshore markets for direction. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0347 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares turn lower midday, led by banks, brokerages on profit-taking, says First Capital analyst; Shanghai Composite Index down 1.3% at 2964.97, support tipped at 2950. Adds "the September economic data meet market expectations and are less likely to trigger another round of tightening moves in the short term." \uc2 Shenzhen Development Bank\uc2  (000001.SZ) off 3.2% at CNY18.85 after 20% gain since start of month, \uc2 China Merchants Bank\uc2  (600036.SH) off 3.6% at CNY15.16 after 21% rise over same period. Citic Securities (600030.SH) down 2.8% at CNY15.54 following more than 50% surge since start of month, Changjiang Securities (000783.SZ) down 2.9% at CNY15.21 after similar gain over same period. Shenzhen Index down 0.3% at 1236.49. (rose.yu@dowjones.com) [ 10-21-10 0037ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101021e6al00037\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
