r whether local authorities might take measures to curb KRW's recent steep gains, traders say. "I think it's going to be a 1,120-1,130 range," for a while now, says local bank trader. "It's very tiring. Government officials are talking everywhere, echoing talks that more restrictions will be implemented soon...we don't know which comments we should pay attention to," says foreign bank trader. Government denies local report that it would announce further measures in early December aimed at curbing speculative foreign capital flows. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0417 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex +0.6% at 19,97.50 in early trade, helped by overnight gains in U.S. markets (DJIA ended +1.2%), short-covering in some bluechips. Market breadth positive with 26 of 30 index issues higher; TCS (532540.BY) +1.5% at INR977.50 before 2Q results later today. "The start is on expected lines. With the index - down over 800 points in previous 5 sessions some buying is happening in select heavyweights," says local dealer. Adds, short-covering helping metal plays. Tips resistance at 20,080, support at 19,850. \uc2 Tata Steel\uc2  (500470.BY) +1.4% at INR624.25, \uc2 Tata Motors\uc2  (500570.BY) +1.4% at INR1,172. \uc2 Reliance Industries\uc2  (500325.BY) +1.2% at INR1,061.50, Sterlite (500900.BY) +1.2% at INR172.30. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0408 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD weaker following U.S. Treasury Secretary Geithner's comment in WSJ interview, China data; pair likely to consolidate in 0.7400-0.7535 range ahead of G-20 this weekend, says \uc2 HIFX\uc2  director of trading Mike Hollows; "The market got unsettled when Geithner made some comments about the value of the U.S. dollar against the euro, the yen and other." Geithner told WSJ that he believes major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are "roughly in alignment now." Says China data did not have much impact "as it was there or there about" expectations. Adds, little top tier data due out over