 though, market also likely to watch weekly primary price data (due later in session) very carefully for signs that inflation may remain far from ebbing, fresh uptick may stoke talk of further RBI rate hikes beginning from next policy meeting on November 2. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0347 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 near flat at 4624.0, sets intraday low of 4622.0 from early high of 4661.4 as offshore markets lose steam. Shanghai Composite down about 1.0% after China economic data. AUD/USD down 0.4%, pointing negatively for risk assets. Traders attribute most of retreat to disappointment that China's GDP, industrial production data didn't "shoot the lights out" since hopes were high after China unexpectedly raised rates. "China data wasn't as strong as the bulls were hoping and we have just rolled over with the rest of Asia," says senior trader at \uc2 UBS\uc2 . "There was obviously some expectation yesterday that China's growth would be very strong." Adds that long-only funds aren't selling out, yet there's "plenty of nervousness around the banks in particular. It's more the fact there's a fight for deposits, so margins are under pressure." Major banks down 0.4%-1.2%. Resources remain positive, though they set intraday lows after the data. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0347 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ shares +0.5% at 3259.17 after market "got a boost on the back of positive movements in markets overnight," says \uc2 Craigs Investment Partners\uc2  broker Belinda Stanley. Fisher & Paykel Healthcare (FPH.NZ) +1.9% at NZ$3.25, may be benefiting from weaker NZD/USD, Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ) +0.7% at NZ$8.19 after positive migration data earlier in day. Index weighed by Telecom (TEL.NZ) down 0.5% at NZ$2.02. Says recent selling in Telecom may be international investors taking advantage of high NZD/USD to repatriate some funds. Notes lack of local drivers means investors will remain focused on offshore markets for direction. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0347 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares turn lower midday, led by banks, brokerages on profit-taking, says First Capital analyst; Shanghai Composite Index down 1.3% at 2964.97, support tipped at 2950. Adds "the September economic data meet market expectations and are less likely to trigger another round of tightening moves in the short term." \uc2 Shenzhen Development Bank\uc2  (000001.SZ) off 3.2% at CNY18.85 after 20% gain since start of month, \uc2 China Merchants Bank\uc2  (600036.SH) off 3.6% at CNY15.16 after 21% rise over same period. Citic Securities (600030.SH) down 2.8% at CNY15.54 following more than 50% surge since start of month, Changjiang Securities (000783.SZ) down 2.9% at CNY15.21 after similar gain over same period. Shenzhen Index down 0.3% at 1236.49. (rose.yu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0343 GMT [Dow Jones] Lowest accepted price for 20-year JGBs for 0345 GMT auction likely around 100.30, traders say. Latest issue carries coupon of 1.8%, lower than previous issue's 1.9%. Lead December futures +0.04 at 143.67. (kazuhiro.shimamura@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0341 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea government bonds, bond futures treading water after abrupt pull-back yesterday on surprise China rate hike; 3-year yield +1 bp at 3.27%, 10-year yield flat at 4.14%, December futures +3 ticks at 112.69. Bond manager at local securities firm expects bond futures to remain within 20-tick range of current levels amid lack of decisive leads. But notes, "bonds are currently very sensitive to issues regarding the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate, a possible tax on foreigners' local bond investments and anything regarding foreign investment in the local market," as any development regarding such issues could lead to choppy trade. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com) [ 10-21-10 0026ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101021e6al00035\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\p