\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ UPDATE: IMF's Lipsky Says China Rate Hike Will Help Balance Economy\b0\par\par\uc2 782 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Oktober 2010\par 07:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Adds further comments from Lipsky, Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, details of Lipsky's meeting with Japan Finance Minister Noda, further background.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Andrew Monahan \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--A top International Monetary Fund official said Wednesday that he welcomed the decision by Chinese authorities Tuesday to raise interest rates, saying it would help Beijing's efforts to balance its goal of continued high growth with its desire to avoid inflation.\par \par \uc2 "It was in line with their general policies of producing better balance in the economy," IMF Deputy Managing Director John Lipsky told reporters during a visit to Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 China's government has reined in liquidity this year to keep the country's economy--a key engine of global growth--from overheating. Late Tuesday, the People's Bank of China took its latest step toward more tightening with a surprise announcement that it was raising key interest rates by a quarter of a percentage point.\par \par \uc2 In January, the PBOC raised overall bank reserve requirements, marking the start of new tightening efforts to prevent the formation of asset bubbles.\par \par \uc2 Still, China's consumer price index is expected to rise 3.6% from a year earlier in September, according to the median forecast of 14 economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires. The index rose 3.5% in August, up from July's 3.3% increase, putting pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's official target of 3% average inflation for the full year.\par \par \uc2 Lipsky's comments come a day after the World Bank made the case that Beijing could fight inflation through other means, such as by letting the yuan appreciate further. "A gradual rise" in the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's nominal exchange rate could "help stem price pressures," the Washington-based international financial institution said in a report issued Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Asked whether China should let the yuan rise more, Lipsky said, "the Chinese authorities have already announced a policy of increased \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility as part of a broad approach toward rebalancing the economy."\par \par \uc2 Since China decided to let the yuan float more freely in June, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has risen 2.4% against the dollar in nominal terms. Critics have said the rise is too modest.\par \par \uc2 But Lipsky expressed patience, saying the policy of greater yuan flexibility is "an ongoing process."\par \par \uc2 Lipsky was speaking to reporters at Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , where he met Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda to discuss the Japanese economy and the government's economic policy, including its downgrade on the country's economy in a monthly report issued Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 According to a \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official, Lipsky welcomed the Bank of Japan's move earlier this month to ease monetary policy further. In a step it billed as "comprehensive easing," Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut its policy rate to a 0.0%-0.10% range, from 0.1%, and announced a new Y5 trillion fund to buy various financial assets, including government and corporate debt.\par \par \uc2 Lipsky would not comment when asked whether he discussed \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues with Noda, but a Finance Ministry official said there was no detailed exchange on the topic.\par \par \uc2 "We had a very cordial, broad-based discussion of the economic and financial landscape," Lipsky said.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy has been a point of intense international interest since Japan dumped an estimated Y2 trillion last month to weaken the soaring yen in its first \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in more than six years. IMF Managing Director Dominique Strauss-Kahn said last month that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other countries "have nothing to gain" by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues are expected to be a topic of discussion at a meeting of finance ministers from the Group of 20 advanced and developing economies in South Korea later this week, as well as at a G-20 leaders summit in Seoul next month.\par \par \uc2 Lipsky suggested that the meetings may not include any specific discussion on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The G-20 will address the framework for strong, sustainable and balanced growth in the mutual assessment process, and that's a broad-based process to examine all facets of policies that are needed to produce the goals of strong, sustainable and balanced growth," Lipsky said. "So we will not single out one aspect."\par \par \uc2 In discussions of any individual country's policies, it is important to consider "the breadth and coherence of the policy efforts that are needed to achieve success in which all G-20 members are participating," Lipsky added.\par \par \uc2 -By Andrew Monahan, Dow Jones Newswires; 81-3-6269-2783; andrew.monahan@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 20, 2010 01:54 ET (05:54 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020101020e6ak000xn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppic