\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ UPDATE: Japan Says Economy Weakening As Strong Yen Hits\b0\par\par\uc2 712 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Oktober 2010\par 11:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Adds background, analyst comment.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Mochizuki \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The Japanese government officially downgraded its view on the economy for the first time in over a year and half, as a strong yen and lower demand from elsewhere in Asia begins to cut into the core industrial sector.\par \par \uc2 "The economic momentum appears to be entering a lull," the government said in its monthly economic report issued Tuesday, downgrading the assessment for the first time since February 2009.\par \par \uc2 Briefing reporters on the results, Minister of State for Economic and Fiscal Policy Banri Kaieda cautioned that "the October-December period will be difficult."\par \par \uc2 The report is the latest indication that Japan's export-dependent economy is showing signs of weakness, although officials still contend that a modest growth path remains in place.\par \par \uc2 Cabinet Office officials said that a slowdown in the Chinese economy, which damaged business activity in other Asian nations and in turn reduced demand for Japan-made products, played a large part in the downgrade.\par \par \uc2 "Shipments have been weakening recently, and the latest data clearly showed industrial output has worsened," one official said.\par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities have becoming increasingly fretful about the economy ever since the yen started a seemingly inexorable rise against the dollar. Since early June, the yen has risen 14% and is now near its all-time high of Y79.75 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 "If the strong yen persists for a comparatively long period of time, that would be a very serious problem," Kaieda said.\par \par \uc2 Government officials, the Bank of Japan and private sector corporate leaders have all warned that the yen's strength will have a serious impact. Not only does a stronger yen make it more difficult for Japanese exporters to compete, it also reduces the value of the overseas earnings when brought back to Japan.\par \par \uc2 Japan has tried to slow down the rise through a number of steps. In mid-September it undertook a wave of yen selling on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market with record sales of Y2 trillion in a single day.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , meanwhile has introduced new easing measures designed to push down interest rates and thereby make the yen a less-attractive investment. It also launched a Y5 trillion fund to purchase government bonds and -- for the first time -- private-sector assets such as ETFs and real estate investment trusts.\par \par \uc2 In its latest forecast in early October, the BOJ also downgraded its own assessment of the economy for the first time since January 2009, citing mounting uncertainty over the global economic outlook and the impact of the yen. A regional survey by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week found that some regions were already starting to see a slowdown.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday's government report lowered its assessment for exports for the second straight month as well as for industrial output, which was revised downward in August. According to the government's most recent data, Japan's exports fell 5.6% in August from a month earlier. Output posted its third straight on-month drop by losing 0.5% in August, and is expected to decline further in September and October.\par \par \uc2 Despite the glum figures, the government said it is hopeful that the economic lull will be temporary and that the government's measures, plus improving overseas economies, will again put growth back on track.\par \par \uc2 A key area of discussion among analysts is whether Japan will again step into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to curb the yen's rise as it did in mid-September, but the monthly report did not touch on the subject of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The report also highlighted \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's high unemployment rate, which stood at 5.1% in August. Prime Minister Naoto Kan has stressed that creating jobs is a key to helping to fuel domestic demand to boost the economy.\par \par \uc2 Through Japan's deflationary period dating back to 1995, workers have seen pay levels squeezed and permanent positions replaced by temporary contracts. Economists say that this has made individuals reluctant to increase spending for fear about their long-term future.\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Mochizuki, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2782; takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 19, 2010 05:18 ET (09:18 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020101019e6aj001pk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
