\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/TWD Up Late; 30.78-30.88 Tipped Wednesday\b0\par\par\uc2 1,232 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Oktober 2010\par 10:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0844 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD ends up 30.900 vs 30.885 yesterday, off intraday low of 30.680. Traders says \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  suspected of buying USD late in day to boost pair after Taiwan \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor Perng Fai-nan says it will continue to keep \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market in check, as hot money flows are disrupting economy. "But we have seen some consolidation as the U.S. dollar has rebounded a bit in international markets," tips local trader. Adds, expects pressure on pair to fall will continue, tips pair to trade in 30.78-30.88 range tomorrow. (paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0843 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  expects Hungarian interest rates to remain on hold at 5.25% until 3Q 2011 when economic recovery becomes firmer and the need for monetary stimulus disappears. However, if markets turn unsatisfied with the government's fiscal package and this creates upward pressure on the EUR/HUF, the Monetary Policy Council "may find itself between a rock and a hard place," says economist Piotr Kalisz. "On one hand, weak economic performance would suggest a need for low interest rates and, on the other hand, financial stability concerns would call for higher rates." Nevertheless, Kalisz expects the MPC will vote for a rate hike only if financial stability concerns became high, which isn't the case at the moment. (margit.feher@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0843 GMT [Dow Jones] Treasurys are steady in London ahead of a long list of Fed speakers lined up on Tuesday, including Chairman Bernanke at 2000 GMT. The markets will also be eyeing comments from Chicago Fed's Evans, New York Fed's Dudley, Dallas Fed's Fisher, and Atlanta Fed's Lockhart. Amid the long list of speakers, housing starts and building permits data are also out at 1230 GMT. The 2-year note is little changed at 100-01/32 to yield 0.353%, according to Tradeweb. The 10-year note is slightly higher at 101-01+/32 to yield 2.503%. The 30-year bond is up at 98-25+/32, yielding 3.943%. December bunds are flat at 130.56. December gilts are also unchanged at 124.44. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0842 GMT [Dow Jones] Two day's running now GBP/USD has survived tests of the 1.5840-20 support area notes \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . While this prop holds a return to 1.60 and Friday's 1.6110 peak is likely says the bank. However a daily close below 1.5820 would probably signal a more protracted decline into month end. GBP/USD now at 1.5848. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0840 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 MKB Bank\uc2  expects yields at Hungary's three-month Treasury bill auction to come in at 5.25%-5.30% amid average demand. This compares to 5.24% at the previous auction a week ago. Hungary's debt agency will auction HUF40 billion ($201.7 million) in three-month T-bills, with results due at 0930 GMT. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0837 GMT [Dow Jones] China onshore IRS up with curve steepening on step-up in inflation expectations on market view U.S. may take further quantitative easing moves. 1-year steady at 2.15%-2.22%, 5-year +2 bps at 3.09%-3.15%, 1-to-5-year spread 2 bps wider at 93 bps. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (PBOC) will hike interest rates some time as the inflation pressure is picking up. So we expect the long-term rates will go higher as if it doesn't hike interest rates now, it will have to hike rates in the future." says Shanghai-based foreign bank trader. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0829 GMT [Dow Jones] European sovereign CDS spreads are steady in early trading Tuesday, with the iTraxx SovX Western European index trading little changed at 137.5bps, according to Markit. Ireland is the biggest gainer with its 5-year CDS spreads narrowing 3bps to 395bps, Markit data showed. Meanwhile, Portugal's 5-year CDS spreads are 3bps tighter at 345bps, Italy's are 1bps tighter at 167bps and Spain's CDS are 1bps wider at 199bps. (clare.connaghan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0827 GMT [Dow Jones] The dollar is in better shape pretty much across the board Tuesday after late comments Monday from US Treasury Secretary Geithner that the US will not encourage devaluation. In Asia the dollar was helped by a less hawkish than expected set of RBA minutes and the Japanese government downgrading its view on the economy, blaming the rising yen and slowing exports. The dollar is up at Y81.40, the euro is down at $1.3918 and the pound is down at 1.5840. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0827 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's avoidance of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since last month may be partly due to worries that further action could add to Washington's frustrations. Though U.S. officials mostly quiet, they were unhappy about Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month as it came at time when they were trying to persuade China to adopt market-oriented \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates. This may require Japan to be cautious about acting again; should U.S. begin to openly grumble about Tokyo action, that could weaken impact of potential \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as signs of policy split between two nations tend to embolden JPY-buying speculators. Also, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's September \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  apparently done without U.S. approval, source says; Tokyo lawmakers tried to show leadership to Japanese people irritated by government's inability to stop JPY rises. See \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: Further \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Risks Washington's Ire. (takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com )\par \par \uc2 0825 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/\uc2 NOK\uc2  is set to rise further as support at 8.13 is seen spurring further gains, says SEB. EUR/\uc2 NOK\uc2  may move towards tests of 8.1860 and 8.2350, the bank adds, after a "stellar performance" Monday restored a "bullish confidence". EUR/\uc2 NOK\uc2  trades at 8.1710.(charles.duxbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0825 GMT [Dow Jones] The zloty edges down as investors continue to take profit following a rapid strengthening in the last ten days, a dealer says. He adds that the EUR/PLN is seen trading in a 3.90-3.94 range Tuesday and rising in the longer term. EUR/PLN is at 3.9325 from 3.9235 late Monday, USD/PLN at 2.8231 from 2.8175. Average yield on 2-year bonds is at 4.73% from 4.75% late Monday, 5-year at 5.22% from 5.26%, and 10-year at 5.60% from 5.58%. (marynia.kruk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0824 GMT [Dow Jones] The decline in excess liquidity in the euro-zone banking system is now impacting EONIA, says KBC Bank. The bank says that the EONIA rate, which jumped higher last Tuesday, the end of the previous reserve maintenance period, didn't drop again once the new reserve period is underway, contrary to previous experience. It says that banks are frontloading less in the new reserve period which limits the room for EONIA to decline further out in the reserve period, unless banks were to start to demand more liquidity again. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com) [ 10-19-10 0444ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101019e6aj0005g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
