\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Hungary 3-Mo T-Bill Yields Seen Around 5.25%-5.3%\b0\par\par\uc2 1,300 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Oktober 2010\par 10:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0840 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 MKB Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  expects yields at Hungary's three-month Treasury bill auction to come in at 5.25%-5.30% amid average demand. This compares to 5.24% at the previous auction a week ago. Hungary's debt agency will auction HUF40 billion ($201.7 million) in three-month T-bills, with results due at 0930 GMT. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0837 GMT [Dow Jones] China onshore IRS up with curve steepening on step-up in inflation expectations on market view U.S. may take further quantitative easing moves. 1-year steady at 2.15%-2.22%, 5-year +2 bps at 3.09%-3.15%, 1-to-5-year spread 2 bps wider at 93 bps. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (PBOC) will hike interest rates some time as the inflation pressure is picking up. So we expect the long-term rates will go higher as if it doesn't hike interest rates now, it will have to hike rates in the future." says Shanghai-based foreign bank trader. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0829 GMT [Dow Jones] European sovereign CDS spreads are steady in early trading Tuesday, with the iTraxx SovX Western European index trading little changed at 137.5bps, according to Markit. Ireland is the biggest gainer with its 5-year CDS spreads narrowing 3bps to 395bps, Markit data showed. Meanwhile, Portugal's 5-year CDS spreads are 3bps tighter at 345bps, Italy's are 1bps tighter at 167bps and Spain's CDS are 1bps wider at 199bps. (clare.connaghan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0827 GMT [Dow Jones] The dollar is in better shape pretty much across the board Tuesday after late comments Monday from US Treasury Secretary Geithner that the US will not encourage devaluation. In Asia the dollar was helped by a less hawkish than expected set of RBA minutes and the Japanese government downgrading its view on the economy, blaming the rising yen and slowing exports. The dollar is up at Y81.40, the euro is down at $1.3918 and the pound is down at 1.5840. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0827 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's avoidance of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since last month may be partly due to worries that further action could add to Washington's frustrations. Though U.S. officials mostly quiet, they were unhappy about Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month as it came at time when they were trying to persuade China to adopt market-oriented \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates. This may require Japan to be cautious about acting again; should U.S. begin to openly grumble about Tokyo action, that could weaken impact of potential \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as signs of policy split between two nations tend to embolden JPY-buying speculators. Also, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's September \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  apparently done without U.S. approval, source says; Tokyo lawmakers tried to show leadership to Japanese people irritated by government's inability to stop JPY rises. See \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: Further \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Risks Washington's Ire. (takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com )\par \par \uc2 0825 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/\uc2 NOK\uc2  is set to rise further as support at 8.13 is seen spurring further gains, says SEB. EUR/\uc2 NOK\uc2  may move towards tests of 8.1860 and 8.2350, the bank adds, after a "stellar performance" Monday restored a "bullish confidence". EUR/\uc2 NOK\uc2  trades at 8.1710.(charles.duxbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0825 GMT [Dow Jones] The zloty edges down as investors continue to take profit following a rapid strengthening in the last ten days, a dealer says. He adds that the EUR/PLN is seen trading in a 3.90-3.94 range Tuesday and rising in the longer term. EUR/PLN is at 3.9325 from 3.9235 late Monday, USD/PLN at 2.8231 from 2.8175. Average yield on 2-year bonds is at 4.73% from 4.75% late Monday, 5-year at 5.22% from 5.26%, and 10-year at 5.60% from 5.58%. (marynia.kruk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0824 GMT [Dow Jones] The decline in excess liquidity in the euro-zone banking system is now impacting EONIA, says KBC Bank. The bank says that the EONIA rate, which jumped higher last Tuesday, the end of the previous reserve maintenance period, didn't drop again once the new reserve period is underway, contrary to previous experience. It says that banks are frontloading less in the new reserve period which limits the room for EONIA to decline further out in the reserve period, unless banks were to start to demand more liquidity again. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0812 GMT [Dow Jones] With stocks moving off lows, bunds are drifting lower, although they continue to move in a narrow band. The German ZEW survey is next up at 0900 GMT, followed by a string of speakers, starting with ECB President Trichet at 1230 GMT. December bunds are down 0.04 at 130.52. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0809 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares end up, reversing earlier losses as gains in nonferrous metal stocks, rare-earth companies more than offset losses in some steelmakers, property developers after China Daily reported government will reduce quotas for rare earth exports by as much as 30% in 1H11 to protect overexploitation of the metals. "The strength in the metal stocks and some smaller-cap stocks like pharmaceuticals helped to turn the index around in the afternoon session. There is still a lid on liquidity in the market and you can't expect funds to only focus on expensive blue chips like banks indefinitely," says Huang Bin from Gold State Securities. Shanghai Composite Index ends +1.6% at 3001.85, nearly 6-month high, after intraday low at 2931.91. Inner Mongolia Baotou Steel Rare-Earth (Group) Hi-Tech (600111.SH) +1.3% to CNY82.57, Minmetals Development (600058.SH) +1.5% to CNY27.65. Shenzhen Composite Index +3.0% at 1231.70. (esther.fung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0808 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: China's economic growth likely moderately slowed in 3Q with GDP growth likely falling to 9.5%, according to median forecast of 14 economists by Dow Jones, from 2Q's +10.3%, but slowdown likely more moderate than expected a few months ago as crackdown on property speculation and weak economic performance abroad had less impact than feared. "After some jitters in the summer, economic activity seemed to stabilize and more recently, to revive," economist Mark Williams says in note for Capital Economics. According to poll, China's September CPI likely +3.6% on year vs August's +3.5%, threatening \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's official target of 3% average inflation for FY10; September producer price index likely +4.1% on year, down from August's +4.3%; September industrial production growth likely slowed slightly to 13.6% on year from 13.9% in August. Data due Thursday at 0200 GMT. See: =PREVIEW: China 3rd-Quarter GDP Growth Likely Slowed To 9.5% (yajun.zhang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0805 GMT [Dow Jones] The risk events are piling up for sterling notes \uc2 Lloyds Banking Group\uc2 . After hours tonight BOE governor King speaks and Wednesday brings the October BOE MPC meeting minutes and the Government's Comprehensive Spending Review. Beforehand, the 1000 GMT CBI industrial trends survey could attract more attention than normal, having slipped in July a further dip would not be surprising given evidence from other data and a weak survey would give sterling bears more ammunition says the bank. GBP/USD trades at 1.5834, EUR/GBP at 0.8787. (gary.stride@dowjones.com) [ 10-19-10 0440ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101019e6aj0005c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
