2 0805 GMT [Dow Jones] The risk events are piling up for sterling notes \uc2 Lloyds Banking Group\uc2 . After hours tonight BOE governor King speaks and Wednesday brings the October BOE MPC meeting minutes and the Government's Comprehensive Spending Review. Beforehand, the 1000 GMT CBI industrial trends survey could attract more attention than normal, having slipped in July a further dip would not be surprising given evidence from other data and a weak survey would give sterling bears more ammunition says the bank. GBP/USD trades at 1.5834, EUR/GBP at 0.8787. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0803 GMT [Dow Jones] The opposition to France pension reform may lose momentum after Tuesday's developments, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 , as widespread demonstrations are expected across France Tuesday with tensions likely to rise. But notes that strike is losing momentum in the public transport sector while students will go on mid-term vacations this week. Finally, the government has started to tap into 3-months strategic oil reserves. (geraldine.amiel@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0755 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 UBS\uc2  ups its 2010 and 2011 forecasts for copper, aluminum, nickel, zinc and lead "to reflect the unexpected positive impact of persistent US quantitative easing on the global commodity trade." Its 2010 copper forecast rises to $3.38 a pound from $3.17/lb and for 2011 to $3.68/lb from $3.45/lb. \uc2 UBS\uc2 's 2010 aluminum forecast rises to $0.98/lb from $0.94/lb and 2011 to $1.11/lb from $1.04/lb. 2010 nickel forecast rises to $9.80/lb from $9.25/lb and 2011 to $9.55/lb from $9.30/lb, 2010 zinc to $0.98/lb from $0.93/lb and 2011 to $1.05/lb from $1.04/lb. 2010 lead forecast rises at $0.95/lb from $0.91/lb and 2011 to $1.01/lb from $1/lb. Longer-term base metals forecasts are not changed. (andrea.hotter@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0751 GMT [Dow Jones] October HSI futures +1.2% at 23,760, rising more than cash market +1.1%. Contract trading at consistent premium