\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: EUR/USD Rises On Tsy Yld Declines, RBA Minutes\b0\par\par\uc2 1,380 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Oktober 2010\par 03:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0107 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD rises to morning high of 1.4005, erasing falls after comments from U.S. Treasury Secretary Geithner, who said U.S. won't try to push down USD for trade advantage. Investors not keen to bet on USD after U.S. Treasury yields decline overnight, says Kenichiro Ikezawa, senior dealer at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Daiwa SB Investments\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Benchmark 10-year yield down 7.9 bps to 2.488%. Says EUR/USD biased up on broad USD weakness on expectation of additional quantitative easing from Fed, may trade in 1.3830-1.4100 band before G-20 meeting later this week. EUR also boosted as RBA, which left interest rate unchanged in October, says it can't wait indefinitely before it hikes again. EUR/JPY rises in tandem to morning high of 113.65 vs last 113.50. AUD/JPY rises to morning high of 80.87 after release of RBA minutes; cross last 80.67. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0058 GMT [Dow Jones] Strong corporate interest to sell USD/SGD stand at 1.3080, while local bids eyed at 1.2970, says Singapore-based dealer. USD/SGD last quoted in interbank market at 1.2989-1.2994. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0055 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares likely to extend yesterday's 1.8% loss due mainly to selling pressure on Apple's (AAPL) contract makers, heavyweight Hon Hai (2317.TW), Quanta (2382.TW), says Taiwan International Securities' analyst Hanna Chang. Tips Taiex could test 7960 floor vs yesterday's 8060.54 close. Notes any technical rebound in near-term likely to meet resistance at 8132. But doesn't expect "any major downturn" as trading volume not contracting drastically; yesterday's trading volume at NT$113.70 billion. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0054 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY likely to fall moderately, as USD weakens vs major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in early Asia trade despite overnight gains; central parity rate tipped around 6.6500 vs 6.6541 yesterday, pair likely to trade in 6.6400-6.6500 band on OTC vs 6.6441 last close. "The dollar didn't strengthen much overnight," says Shanghai-based trader at foreign bank; notes "in fact it has weakened so far in Asia with the dollar index at 77.019, down from around 77.50 in late Asia yesterday." Dollar index at 77.184 late NY. Adds to closely monitor fixing for policy cue; "it's possible the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will let the yuan consolidate around current levels after its strong gains in recent weeks." (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0048 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares may rise following gains in U.S. stocks, LME metals overnight. Shanghai Composite Index may test 3000 resistance again after last ending down 0.5% at 2955.23. "The fact that the stock index didn't fall significantly after recent strong gains suggests the market is supported by strong demand," says Qian Qimin at Shenyin Wanguo Securities. Shenzhen index ends down 1.4% at 1196.23. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0046 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD up tad as RBA signals more rate hikes are coming. AUD/USD recently at 0.9940 vs 0.9925 just prior to release of minutes from RBA's October meeting, when it surprised market by keeping rates steady at 4.5%. Looking to November, RBA made clear in minutes that it can't wait indefinitely before hiking again -- a clear bullish signal for AUD/USD cross given U.S. Fed continues to hint at possible quantitative easing, though concerns remain for RBA. "To me, these minutes say they are much more focused on the global outlook than domestic data. On timing, they want clarity on QEII and that comes after their November meeting," says Roland Randall, strategist with TD. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0046 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi last +0.4% at 1883.47 as foreigners, retail investors buy, with autos, financials, shipbuilding stocks offering good support. Still, tech stocks weak, weighing on broad market; "investors are disappointed at Apple's lower-than-expected first quarter (from October to December) earnings forecasts despite the company's strong third quarter results," says Bae Sung-young at \uc2 Hyundai Securities\uc2 ; tips resistance at 1890. Foreigners net buyers today, picking up KRW25.3 billion worth of local stocks. Retail investors buying KRW56 billion worth, while domestic institutions selling net KRW22 billion. \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) down 0.4% at KRW748,000, LG Display (034220.SE) off 1.0% at KRW40,500, \uc2 Samsung SDI\uc2  (006400.SE) down 2.9% at KRW148,500. Among gainers, \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) +2.0% at KRW157,000, \uc2 Industrial Bank of Korea\uc2  (024110.SE) +2.1% at KRW16,900, \uc2 Hyundai Heavy Industries\uc2  (009540.SE) +2.0% at KRW337,000. (kyong-ae.choi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0045 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP tipped to move lower in line with EUR/USD advance, expected stream of inflows from overseas Filipino workers, overnight gains of U.S. equities. Pair may head towards 43.15 support from 43.23 late Monday as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to temper PHP's appreciation, says local bank trader; "the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to protect the 43.15 support. I think they prefer the exchange rate to remain at this level for the meantime." Tips next resistance at 43.30.(rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0045 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD likely down vs 30.885 late yesterday as foreign funds expected to flow into local stock market, tracking Wall Street's overnight gains, early strength in Japan, Korean stock markets, says foreign bank trader. Tips pair in 30.700-30.800 band for session; "TWD is still on an uptrend, supported by abundant liquidity." (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0038 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI may rise, buoyed by gains in U.S. stocks overnight; but dearth of fresh developments on domestic front likely to keep key index confined to narrow ranges near term, dealers say. "The market can be expected to remain in consolidation mode, within the 1475 to 1490 range, as investors wait for new drivers," dealer says. KLCI ends 0.6% lower at 1480.70 yesterday. Konsortium Logistik (6157.KU) may be in focus after state-owned private equity fund Ekuiti Nasional bought 56.5% stake for MYR206.6 million to emerge as firm's new major shareholder. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0038 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei +0.7% at 9564.81, after opening in negative territory, on rebound in financial shares following overnight gains in U.S. peers on brisk 3Q earnings by \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  (C). Masayoshi Yano, senior market analyst at Meiwa Securities, says stocks rising despite continuing weakness in USD/JPY (now 81.19) due to excess liquidity flowing into markets. "If the yen strengthens further, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  should carry out an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market) and further quantitative easing measures, which would then prevent stocks from falling further," he adds. Puts today's Nikkei range at 9400-9600, noting investor sentiment slightly soured by sharp falls in Apple (AAPL) and \uc2 International Business Machines\uc2  (IBM) in after-hours trading despite posting better-than-expected quarterly results. 31/33 Topix subindexes higher. \uc2 Mitsubishi UFJ Financial Group\uc2  (8306.TO) up 1.3% at Y387, \uc2 Mizuho Financial Group (8411.TO) \uc2 up 1.7% at Y119. \uc2 KDDI\uc2  (9433.TO) up 2.0% at Y407,000 on tie-up with Skype. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0036 GMT [Dow Jones] RBA's October meeting minutes clearly spell out policy makers are itching to tighten rates, only question is when. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says decision to stand pat this month was "finely balanced" and it can't wait "indefinitely" to see whether risks on global stage materialize before hiking. Clear message from minutes is new information before the November meeting, with 3Q CPI due Oct. 27, could be enough to push bank over the line. Also of note are comments on AUD strength, which bank says will help contain inflation. Little new there, but does illustrate RBA is comfortable with AUD's ongoing strength. (enda.curran@dowjones.com) [ 10-18-10 2107ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101019e6aj0000q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
