\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Nikkei +0.7% As Banks Gain On \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  Earnings\b0\par\par\uc2 1,585 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Oktober 2010\par 02:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0038 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei +0.7% at 9564.81, after opening in negative territory, on rebound in financial shares following overnight gains in U.S. peers on brisk 3Q earnings by \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  (C). Masayoshi Yano, senior market analyst at Meiwa Securities, says stocks rising despite continuing weakness in USD/JPY (now 81.19) due to excess liquidity flowing into markets. "If the yen strengthens further, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  should carry out an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market) and further quantitative easing measures, which would then prevent stocks from falling further," he adds. Puts today's Nikkei range at 9400-9600, noting investor sentiment slightly soured by sharp falls in Apple (AAPL) and \uc2 International Business Machines\uc2  (IBM) in after-hours trading despite posting better-than-expected quarterly results. 31/33 Topix subindexes higher. \uc2 Mitsubishi UFJ Financial Group\uc2  (8306.TO) up 1.3% at Y387, \uc2 Mizuho Financial Group (8411.TO) \uc2 up 1.7% at Y119. \uc2 KDDI\uc2  (9433.TO) up 2.0% at Y407,000 on tie-up with Skype. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0036 GMT [Dow Jones] RBA's October meeting minutes clearly spell out policy makers are itching to tighten rates, only question is when. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says decision to stand pat this month was "finely balanced" and it can't wait "indefinitely" to see whether risks on global stage materialize before hiking. Clear message from minutes is new information before the November meeting, with 3Q CPI due Oct. 27, could be enough to push bank over the line. Also of note are comments on AUD strength, which bank says will help contain inflation. Little new there, but does illustrate RBA is comfortable with AUD's ongoing strength. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0036 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares likely to rise, tracking gains across regional bourses after DJIA ends at highest level since May 3, helped by upbeat earnings from \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 ; that actual earnings results fueled buying interest, rather than Fed easing hopes, also viewed as positive; "the quantitative easing story has had a good run, and now people will want to look at earnings, both here and in the U.S. for more reasons to buy," says dealer at local brokerage. M1 (B2F.SG) - first telco to report 3Q earnings - likely in focus, may underperform as costs rise 34.5% on year. \uc2 Global Logistic Properties\uc2  (MC0.SG) also likely to remain in focus after robust debut yesterday, closing 11% above IPO price, with around 466 million shares traded - around a fifth of total market volume. STI ends down 0.7% at 3,181.27, with 344 losers/161 gainers; May 2008 peak of 3,267 likely to remain near-term cap. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0030 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW lower at 1,115.5 vs 1,119.3 late yesterday in Seoul as EUR pushing higher in early Asian hours, lifting KRW, following Wall Street gains, analysts say. Woori Futures analyst Byeon Ji-young expects pair's trade to be largely focused around low-to-mid-1,110s area. Notes, "the downward momentum in the pair isn't as strong as it had been last week," with broad USD weakness taking breather in global market. Adds, "though the euro is strengthening, it is likely to meet resistance again at the $1.4000 mark. So long as the euro doesn't break through that level, there will be no strong driver to lead the local pair to breach the 1,110 support." EUR/USD last at 1.3973. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0028 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR at 3.0900, unchanged from late yesterday; pair's trading range of 3.0800-3.1000, which has prevailed for past 1 week, likely to persist today in subdued trading in absence of fresh catalysts, dealer says; adds, with 2011 budget unveiled recently providing no new cues, MYR expected to continue tracking other major regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  near term. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0013 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore government bond prices likely to rise tracking overnight gains in treasurys, but fall in yields won't be much, says trader with foreign bank. Adds "people are long bonds as worries still remain over the pace of global economic recovery." Tips yields to fall between 2-3 bps today. Yesterday 2-year bond yield was unchanged at 0.40%, while 10-year rose 2 bps to end 1.98%. (venkat.pr@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0009 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead December JGB futures open slightly lower as investors tread carefully ahead of 5-year JGB auction (results due 0345 GMT); contract last down 0.05 at 143.83. Deutsche Securities strategist Makoto Yamashita expects 5-year JGB auction to meet solid demand despite expected low coupon as players seeking place to park surplus cash. Tips JGB yield curve to remain steep, with medium- to long-term JGBs keeping strong momentum. 10-year cash bonds yet to change hands; last closed at 0.870%. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0008 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude likely to consolidate for now after rebounding nearly $3 from 6-session low $80.35 yesterday, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. Daily continuation chart mixed as stochastics falling from overbought level, but MACD still in bullish mode, while 5-day moving average meandering sideways. Immediate resistance at $83.33 (Friday's high); breach would target $84.12 (Thursday's high), then $84.43 (5-month high set October 7) - above which would open way up to psychological round-numbered levels of $85.00, $86.00, $87.00, then $87.15 (May 3 peak). Support at $81.55 (hourly chart); breach would expose downside to $80.35-$80.30 band (yesterday's low-Oct. 8 reaction low), then $79.70 (Oct. 1 low), $78.04 (previous cap set September 13, near 200-day moving average) and $77.53 (55-day moving average). November crude last down 1 cent at $83.07/bbl on Globex. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0007 GMT [Dow Jones] Crude oil futures rose Monday with U.S. stocks on optimism about broader economy and worries about the effects of prolonged strikes in France. November Nymex crude settled up $1.83, or 2.3%, at $83.08/bbl; November ICE Brent crude settled up $1.92 higher at $84.37; DJIA up 0.7%. Oil prices rebounded from $80.30 technical support level, aided by higher stocks and data showing U.S. home-builder confidence rose in October for first time in 5 months; worker strikes in France have disrupted or halted production at the country's 12 refineries, providing additional support for crude. November Nymex crude recently down 4 cents at $83.04. (cassandra.sweet@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0006 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/SGD last bid 1.2998 vs 1.3052 late Asia yesterday on fund flows, investors chasing high yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  given positive outlook towards Asian region, says trader with foreign bank. "The market is clear that the Fed will eventually take more quantitative easing measures which is (U.S.) dollar bearish and will be supportive of inflows in Asia," says trader. Tips support at 1.2950, resistance at 1.3020. (venkat.pr@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2357 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysian government bonds may extend declines amid persistent concerns over expected increase in bond issuances in 2011. Analysts estimate total MYR-denominated government bond issuances of MYR80 billion-MYR90 billion in 2011 vs estimated MYR58 billion in 2010, with about half the amount mainly for redemption of maturing bonds; they say issuances likely skewed towards longer-end of yield curve, which suggests upward bias for longer-dated bonds. "There is some supply concerns at the moment, but we expect any pressure on bonds next year to be only mild because there should be sufficient liquidity to absorb the increased supply," dealer says. Yesterday, yields on benchmark MGSs ended mostly higher with May 2013 down 1 bp at 3.14%, August 2015 +3 bps at 3.25%, September 2017 +2 bps at 3.55%, November 2019 +1 bp at 3.69%. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2352 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 up 0.3% at 4665.4, hits 4668.4 as banks track U.S. peer recovery on strong results from \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 . Broader market benefiting from improvement in risk assets after weak U.S. industrial production bolstered expectations of quantitative easing at the Nov. 2-3 FOMC meeting. Major Aussie banks up 0.2%-0.8%, while miners are mixed, with Fortescue (FMG.AU) up 1.9%, \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU) down 0.6%. S&P/ASX 200 gains are being tempered by afterhours falls in U.S. stock index futures, with S&P 500 futures down 0.4%, Nasdaq 100 futures down 1.4%. Follows afterhours reports from IBM, down 3.6% after its 3Q results revealed weaker new services contracts, and Apple down 5.7% on weaker 1Q outlook. Macmahon (MAH.AU) also damping local market sentiment, with shares down 20% after warning of flat 1H profit, due to lower-than-expected work in hand and WA Rail contract expected to miss forecast profit. Traders await Wall Street's reaction to IBM and Apple results, as well as tonight's U.S. housing data and results from market heavyweights, BofA and GS. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com) [ 10-18-10 2038ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101019e6aj0000f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
