\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Stocks To Test Higher; Bernanke Awaited\b0\par\par\uc2 3,230 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Oktober 2010\par 07:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SNAPSHOT: \par ======== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European bourses seen up a bit, govt debt mixed; euro lower, oil futures mixed, spot gold down\f28 \par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Google\uc2 , Seagate, AMD Higher In After Hours Trading\par \par \uc2 -Germany's Merkel: Economy Decisively On Right Track\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  Exceeds 30% Threshold, Holds 30.01% Of Postbank\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 HSBC\uc2  Close To Dropping Bid For South Africa's Nedbank -FT\par \par \uc2 -Daimler, Europcar Set Car Rental Joint Venture -Report\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Panalpina\uc2 , Shell Near Settlement Of Bribery Probe -WSJ\par \par \uc2 -Germany Plans To Block Rio-BHP Iron-Ore Joint Venture\par \par \uc2 Watch for: 0900 GMT EU Aug Foreign Trade; 0900 GMT EU Sep Harmonized CPI; 1200 GMT US Federal Reserve Bank of Atlanta President Dennis Lockhart; 1230 GMT US Oct Empire State Manufacturing Survey; 1230 GMT US Sep Advance Monthly Sales for Retail & Food Services; 1230 GMT US Sep CPI\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK & US/ASIAN SUMMARIES: \par ======== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As commodities cool after recent rallies, Asian stock markets are tipping lower Friday. But European stocks may nudge higher after some bullish tech news out of the U.S. overnight. Government debt may start mixed. The euro is giving back a bit of ground to the dollar, with spot gold lower and oil prices mixed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par STOCKS: \par ====== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European bourses are likely to start slightly higher on Friday, while banking shares remain in focus and technology stocks get a boost from some U.S. earnings reports.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For Friday's opening, IG Markets is calling the FTSE up 7 at 5734, the DAX up 13 at 6460 and the CAC up 1.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Two sector messages seem clear: regulators are going to demand significantly more than 7.0% core Tier 1 (bank capital) under Basel III, and they are not going to let banks meander towards that level over the next nine years," analysts at Macquarie Research said in a note to clients.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The European banking sector faces a "substantial core tier 1 deficit under Basel III," they said. The Tier 1 capital ratio is an important measure of financial strength.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The bigger picture is that we had a great rally in September and into October," said Jonathan Vyorst, senior vice president and portfolio manager at Paradigm Capital Management.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stock futures are higher on Friday, after markets closed modestly lower Thursday as investors retreated from financials because of concerns over banks' foreclosure practices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Financials led the Dow's declines, in particular \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan Chase\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Both are among the wave of banks reviewing their foreclosures after temporarily suspending evictions due to concerns over "robo signers," who approve hundreds of foreclosure documents a day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The big banks that were most involved in not only originating mortgages but the securitization of mortgages are the ones that are most at risk here," said Don Wordell, portfolio manager of the RidgeWorth Mid-Cap Value Fund.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But investors also said the market may also just be taking a natural breather after hitting its highest close since early May on Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In late trading, shares of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Seagate Technology Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . leapt 20%, with the company now in early talks to go private, while \f28 \uc2 \f2 Google Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 .'s share price jumped nearly 10% after the search giant's quarterly results surpassed expectations. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Advanced Micro Devices Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . shares also rose sharply on news the company's quarterly adjusted earnings more than doubled from a year ago.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian stock markets are mostly lower Friday with banking stocks in Seoul and Sydney weighing on those markets, while metal shares fell in Shanghai after recent strong gains.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some investors took cash off the table after Thursday's rally and ahead of Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke's speech later in the global day. Markets will be watching closely for clues on the timing and extent of any further quantitative easing measures to kick start an ailing U.S. economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Further (quantitative easing) by the Federal Reserve in early November is looking increasingly inevitable although doubts remain on the size of the package and its effectiveness," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mark Smith\f28 \uc2 \f2 , economist at ANZ bank in Wellington. "Investors are starting to realise that looser U.S. monetary policy may not be the magic bullet they were hoping for," he said.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \par ====== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro is retreating a bit against the dollar as risk aversion kicks in again, but the dollar's overall weak turn of late remains in force.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "A broader emerging market move against the dollar is starting to become more acceptable," said Steven Englander, head of G10 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  frictions continue to increase although '\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  war' seems to be an extreme label for it," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Agricole Corporate and Investment Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors will be listening closely Friday to Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke's speech at a conference in Boston for clues on the timing and extent of any further quantitative easing measures. Any hints that asset-purchase programs might not be as extensive as once thought would support the greenback, said analysts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sterling is likely to consolidate after setting an eight-month high of $1.6067 yesterday, with negative dollar sentiment providing support. Still, the pound's topside is likely limited by negative domestic UK concerns. Against the euro, sterling is seen in ranges.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's finance minister kept the possibility of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market on the table Friday, saying the government will take "decisive" steps if necessary, after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  touched a 15-year high of Y80.88 to the dollar overnight.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BONDS: \par ===== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bunds and gilts may start mixed as investors weigh the need for safety going into the weekend, although the underlying appeal of sovereign debt remains dodgy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The tightening of peripheral spreads has impacted the safe haven appeal of bunds somewhat. The market is now waiting for Fed Chairman Bernanke's speech on Friday to hear what the FOMC's thoughts on quantitative easing are," says David Schnautz at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The second and final reading of the euro-zone consumer price index for September will likely confirm the preliminary reading of a higher pace of year-on-year inflation, economists say.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The rise in food and energy will help drive a rebound in headline inflation to 1.8% on the year, confirming the flash, and its highest level since the end of 2008," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2  economists.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the UK, "The tightening of gilt yield spreads over bunds has some way to go. Further signs of weakness in the economy will support expectations that the BOE could restart quantitative easing, which will be gilt positive compared to a more hawkish stance being adopted by ECB members," says a London-based strategist.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasurys are higher Friday, after a tepid 30-year bond sale fueled broad selling in the Treasury market Thursday, sending the benchmark 10-year note's yield to the highest level since the start of the month.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The auction erased an earlier rally in the 30-year bond and turned it into the biggest loser in the market. The yield hit its highest level since Sept. 20.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The $13 billion auction garnered the smallest demand since February, wrapping up this week's $66 billion in new government notes and bonds supply.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All three sales for the week, including $32 billion in three-year notes Tuesday and $21 billion in 10-year notes Wednesday, were weaker than recent averages, sending signals that ultralow bond yields have started to push many investors to seek higher returns in other assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Markets remain full of paradoxes," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Societe Generale\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "There remains a strong appetite for risk, but it looks like some problems continue to move inexorably towards some kind of restructuring. And the Fed wants to get inflation expectations up; yet is also happy to see nominal rates contained. And we are seeing good cash buying of bonds on the whole but cheap bonds get even cheaper, with the latest 30-year bond reopening proving weak."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, many market participants are betting that the Fed may buy bonds on a larger scale, with a size estimated between $500 billion and $1 trillion, aimed at pushing down long-term borrowing costs to spur consumer spending and business investments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Certainly, three poorly received auctions in a row piques our interest but we are not yet ready to declare the bond market rally dead," said Dan Greenhaus, chief economic and bond strategist at Miller, Tabak & Co. in New York. "We want to sit through additional auctions before turning hysterical."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A fresh round of asset purchases to stimulate a sagging economy might not help as much as some hope, the president of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve Bank of Minneapolis\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Tokyo on Friday, lead December Japanese government bond futures were down 0.20 at 143.72 points after Treasurys fell on Thursday. The 10-year cash JGB yield was up two basis points at 0.895%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan Governor Masaaki Shirakawa said Friday that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is ready to take appropriate monetary policy measures amid downside risks to the domestic economy such as the rising yen and slowing overseas economies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ENERGY: \par ======= \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil prices are mixed while markets consider the worsening employment numbers in the U.S., which could prompt further monetary easing there.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New York's main contract, light, sweet crude for November delivery, fell four cents to $82.65 a barrel.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brent North Sea crude for delivery in December gained a cent to $84.21 barrel.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par METALS: \par ====== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Technical analysis by ScotiaMocatta suggests that Thursday's kind of price action in gold often signals a technical reversal warning, but the metal needs a lower close today to trigger a sell signal. The firm says market remains bullish toward gold but will likely exit fresh longs on any close back below recent resistance at $1,355, with immediate support at $1,372 and resistance at $1,388. Spot gold was down $2.70 from the New York close at $1,378.50 per troy ounce. [ 15-10-10 0500GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar's slight recovery is causing London Metal Exchange metals to edge lower, pending the Fed chief Bernanke's speech later in the global day and any effects on the dollar. LME 3-month copper is at $8,378/ton, down $22 from Thursday's late kerb.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; dennis.baker@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 15-10-10 0500GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      11094.57   -1.51  -0.01% \par Nasdaq     2435.38   -5.85  -0.24% \par S&P 500    1173.81   -4.29  -0.36% \par FTSE 100   5727.21  -20.14  -0.35% \par Xetra DAX  6455.27  +20.75  +0.32% \par CAC40      3819.17  -9.17   -0.24% \par Above are closing prices \par  \par Nikkei 225   9541.09   -42.42  -0.44% \par Hang Seng    23739.97 -112.20  -0.47% \par S&P/ASX 200   4687.50  -11.59  -0.25% \par Taiwan Index  8221.65  +6.20   +0.08% \par S.Korea Kospi 1899.84  +0.08   +0.00% \par Shanghai Comp 2927.25  +47.62  +1.65% \par Bombay SenSex 20443.13 -54.51  +0.27% \par Dow Future    11056.00 +4.00    0.0% \par NASDAQ Future 2069.25  +7.25   +0.3% \par S&P Future    1173.60  +0.10    0.0% \par Above are as of 0425 GMT \par  \par USD/JPY      81.38-42      -0.07% \par Range        81.62   81.34 \par EUR/USD      1.4042-47    -0.26% \par Range        1.4082  1.4010 \par AUD/USD      0.9915-20    -0.23% \par Range        0.9940  0.9892 \par GBP/USD      1.6000-05    -0.05% \par Range        1.6010  1.5975 \par USD/CHF      0.9545-55    +0.19% \par Range        0.9561  0.9530 \par EUR/GBP      0.8775-78    -0.20% \par Range        0.8796  0.8763 \par \u203\'00\'CBUR/CHF      1.3409-14    -0.05% \par Range        1.3426  1.3386 \par Above are as of 0350 GMT vs NY close \par  \par USD/JPY Vol Option Contract 11.90%/12.40% \par Day