\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Slumps Broadly; Possible Fed Stimulus Weighs\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bradley Davis   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 776 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Oktober 2010\par 14:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar fell broadly against its rivals Friday after flat U.S. consumer prices and a speech by Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke added weight to the case for a fresh round of economic stimulus.\par \par \uc2 U.S. data continue to point to an economy that looks to be on course to avoid a so-called double-dip recession, but one that is registering growth too slow to create jobs and pull steadily and rapidly from its deep recession, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 "The lack of a strong recovery in growth or firm decline in unemployment is definitely forcing the Fed's hand to act sooner rather than later," said David Semmens, U.S. economist at Standard Chartered Bank in New York.\par \par \uc2 A wide swath of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  gained to record highs against the dollar, though their actual moves were relatively modest: The euro rose above $1.4150, touching $1.4161, its highest level since January 2010, while the ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dropped to its lowest level since December 2009. The Australian dollar hit parity with the U.S. dollar for the first time since 1983, when the Aussie was freely floated. The U.K. pound ticked above $1.61 for the first time since January.\par \par \uc2 A fresh round of Fed asset purchases, known as quantitative easing, would weigh on the dollar, with the Fed acting to push down longer-term interest rates to stimulate spending.\par \par \uc2 "Overall economic growth has been proceeding at a pace that is less vigorous than we would like," Bernanke said Friday in prepared text. The Federal Open Market Committee, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rate-setting arm, "is prepared to provide additional accommodation if needed to support the economic recovery and to return inflation over time to levels consistent with our mandate," he said.\par \par \uc2 Friday morning, the euro was at $1.4116 from $1.4077 from late Thursday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y81.20 from Y81.51, after dropping as far as Y80.88, touching the line of its 15-year low. The euro was at Y114.59 from Y114.74. The U.K. pound was at $1.6069 from $1.6013 after gaining to $1.6104. The dollar was at CHF0.9524 from CHF0.9527.\par \par \uc2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was at 76.350 from 76.563.\par \par \uc2 Bernanke stopped short of announcing a new policy, saying as the Fed contemplates doing more, officials "will take account of the potential costs and risks" of pursuing unconventional policies, and anything that is done will be "contingent on incoming information about the economic outlook and financial conditions."\par \par \uc2 U.S. consumer prices barely budged during September in yet another sign the Fed might need to take action to combat continued weakness in the economy amid concerns about low inflation. U.S. retail sales, meanwhile, rose for a third consecutive month in September, posting a stronger-than-expected increase that should fend off fears of a double-dip recession but doesn't signal a strong recovery.\par \par \uc2 The dollar continued its decline against the yen, even as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance minister kept the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on the table Friday, saying the government will take "decisive" steps if necessary.\par \par \uc2 The dollar on Thursday fell to a fresh 15-year low at Y80.88, a level the dollar again dipped to early Friday morning before bouncing slightly back.\par \par \uc2 Separately, China's yuan hit another high under the current system against the U.S. dollar Friday, ahead of an announcement from the U.S. Treasury later Friday on whether to label China as a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   Canada Morning \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Canadian dollar was modestly higher Friday morning as it benefited from broadly based U.S. dollar weakness after Bernanke's speech reaffirmed the possibility of additional quantitative easing in the U.S.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. dollar was at C$1.0040 from C$1.0063 late Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. dollar dropped to a low of C$1.0011, according to CQG, after the text of Bernanke's marks was released, but subsequently rebounded in volatile trading.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Canadian dollar also benefited from news shortly after the Bernanke comments that Canadian manufacturing shipments were up 2.0% in August from July, considerably exceeding the expected 0.5% increase.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Bradley Davis, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2654; bradley.davis@dowjones.com [ 10-15-10 0855ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1033 1074 1158 1863 1865 20714 28038 4164   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101015e6af0008u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
