\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: AUD/JPY, NZD/JPY Down On Profit-Taking -Dealer\b0\par\par\uc2 1,523 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Oktober 2010\par 04:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0249 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/JPY falls to intraday low at 80.50 as investors take profits from this week's gains in AUD after AUD/USD failed to hit parity yesterday, says Kenichiro Ikezawa, senior dealer at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Daiwa SB Investments Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Says mostly weak Asian shares adding to uncertainty over pace of global economic recovery, prompting investors to trim bets on commodity \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  such as AUD, NZD. Also says AUD/JPY may fall to 80.00 in global day, last at 80.61. NZD/JPY drops to intraday low of 61.55, last at 61.62.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0248 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan, Korea next more likely candidates (after Thailand) to tighten capital flows in Asia amid strong inflows to region, says \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 . Notes Taiwan more export dependent and likely less tolerant of TWD appreciation, while Korea has seen sizable inflows into KRW bonds/bills. "If inflows persists, Taiwan could tighten regulations on foreign holdings of fixed income instruments as it initiated in November 2009, as well as encourage more capital outflows. Meanwhile, Korea could tighten ceiling on banks' \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  derivative exposure." USD/TWD last at 30.730 vs 30.770 yesterday, USD/KRW last at 1,115.30 vs 1,110.90. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0244 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea government bonds, bond futures marginally lower in morning trade, taking breather after strong rally yesterday on BOK's decision to keep key policy rate on hold at 2.25% for third straight month; participants say. Shorter-end bonds slightly outperforming longer ones on view \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will stand pat on rates for rest of this year - a view which will likely keep overall bullish sentiment in bond market intact for a while, says bond manager at local securities firm. Adds, "many seem to be anticipating a breach of the 3.00% level for the three-year yields soon." Those yields last quoted +1 bp at 3.09%, 5-year yield +1 bp at 3.46%, 10-year +4 bps at 3.95%, December futures down 5 ticks at 113.34. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0243 GMT [Dow Jones] Possibility BOT to hike policy rate at Wednesday's meeting reduced after Thai PM Abhisit Vejjajiva recently said \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will take THB strength into policy rate's consideration, says Credit Agricole CIB Asia Research. BOT raised policy rate for two straight meetings in July, August to 1.75% after maintaining it at 1.25% since April 2009. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by BOT appears to have stepped up recently; forward book increased to $13 billion on Oct. 1 from $11.1 billion in previous week. Book could have been further expanded during last week (data due later today) amid continued USD weakness. Asian regulators trying to find ways, in addition to direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to curb their local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' strength, including discouraging inflows by taxation, and maintaining low interest rates. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0242 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares down 0.4% at 3603.829, weighed by falls in several Asian markets and amid absence of fresh leads, traders say; tip immediate support at 3600. "It's a healthy correction after recent gains," says trader at First Asia Capital; index still+ 42% since start of 2010. Among decliners: Bank Rakyat (BBRI.JK) down 1.9% at IDR10,550, Bank Danamon (BDMN.JK) down 1.6% at IDR6,000 on profit taking. Meanwhile Bank Negara (BBNI.JK) up 1.2% after reporting strong 9-month earnings. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0241 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot silver at $24.67/oz, up 4 cents since NY close after again outperforming Thursday, rising 2.4% vs gold's 0.6%. \uc2 Macquarie Bank\uc2  says gains "staggering." Silver now +39% vs Aug. 24 low, when it broke out of old range, and just 1% shy of yesterday's intraday, 30-year, high of $24.91. \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  adds: "Physically backed silver exchange traded products (ETPs) rose by 40 tons Wednesday, taking total metal held to a staggering 14,148 tons, with inflows for the month already at 389 tons as retail interest remains ripe," says report. Silver mine supply was 22,000 tons in 2009. Outlook far from clear short term, with many traders viewing market as overextended. Mitsui notes $1,400 for gold, $25 for silver likely to be pivotal levels with large interest in Comex December options strikes. Comex December options expiry on Nov. 23, with precious metals direction likely to rest on Nov. 3 Federal Reserve meeting, when speculation over quantitative easing to finally end. Spot gold at $1,377.90/oz, down $3.30. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0240 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been avoiding JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for 3 main reasons despite fact that pair again below level of just under 83.00 that triggered JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Tokyo on September 15, \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  says. First, ahead of G-20 next weekend, Japan wants to avoid facing criticism as China being forced to allow more CNY appreciation. Second, Nikkei holding up well, suggesting Japan firms not as damaged by high JPY as believed (Nikkei last 9515 vs September 15 close of 9517). Third, JPY strength mostly vs USD, not other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But notes odds of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may rise if pair drops towards all-time low level (official low from BOJ is 79.75 in April 1995), or Nikkei drops sharply. Pair marked fresh 15-year low at 80.88 yesterday vs last 81.39. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0240 GMT [Dow Jones] China attracts $8.4 billion worth of foreign direct investment in September, +6.1% on-year, marking rebound in growth after 2 consecutive months of slowdowns, Ministry of Commerce says. FDI +1.4% in August, +29.2% in July, following 39.6% rise in June. In January-September period, FDI rose 16.6% from year earlier to $74.34 billion, slowing from 18.1% increase during January-August period. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0229 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares lower, with investors taking profit after yesterday's gain, also flat performance on Wall Street overnight; bellwether PSEi off 0.5% at 4214.08 in moderate volume, with decliners trouncing gainers 60 to 37. Immediate support at 4200. PCCI Securities Brokers analyst Paul Balaoing says aside from absence of strong leads, investors also waiting for 3Q results and "reallocating some funds to Cebu Pacific" whose ongoing IPO is country's 2nd-largest ever. Leading losers are Metropolitan Bank & Trust (MBT.PH), down 3.0% to PHP69.70, \uc2 Bank of the Philippine Islands\uc2  (BPI.PH) down 0.4% to PHP56, Alliance Global Group (AGI.PH) down 1.8% to PHP10.98. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0224 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares consolidate after yesterday's 1.3% rise; Taiex last steady at 8212.16, after seesawing in tight range of 8203.32-8230.40. "With tech stocks remaining unfavored, we will stick to our viewpoint of a rangebound pattern and do not believe that a breakout of the pattern in an upward motion will occur in the near term," says Taiwan International Securities' analyst Andrew Teng. But adds, ample liquidity will lend support; tips index in tight 8200-8250 range today. Profit-taking hits chip, PC stocks: TSMC (2330.TW) down 0.5% at NT$61.50, UMC (2303.TW) off 0.4% at NT$13.65, Hon Hai (2317.TW) down 0.4% at NT$115.00, Acer (2353.TW) off 1.1% at NT$78.10. Petrochemical stocks continue rising on strong product prices: Formosa Plastics (1301.TW) +0.5% at NT$82.40, \uc2 Nan Ya Plastics\uc2  (1303.TW) +0.9% at NT$67.30. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0224 GMT [Dow Jones] Natural rubber on Shanghai Futures Exchange higher, bucking trend on SHFE complex, boosted by continued funds buying, solid spot prices, says Shanghai-based trader; adds, technical factors also point to more near-term upside. Notes heavy rain in Hainan province, key producing area, helping trading houses hike prices on physical market, while broad bullish sentiment attracts more speculative funds entering market with long-positions. Tips nearby resistance at CNY32,000/ton on chart-based cues. Benchmark March contract last up 1.3% at CNY31,660/ton. (yue.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0224 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares down 0.6% at 455.89 in thin trade with investors selling on inflation concerns after Ban Viet Securities forecast October CPI to rise between 0.7% and 0.9% on month, making it difficult to meet government's inflation target of 8% this year. "Though some banks have cut interest rates, concerns over high consumer price inflation continues to keep most players on the sidelines," says FPT Securities analyst. Adds buying appetite likely to only rise after companies start issuing 3Q earnings results later this month. Tips key index in 453-460 range. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com) [ 10-14-10 2249ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101015e6af00021\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
