\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ/The Source: Waiting For the Big Bad Japanese Wolf\b0\par\par\uc2 654 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2010\par 11:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (This article has been posted on The Source, the Wall Street Journal Online's site for European real-time analysis {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/source"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/source }}}\uc2 ) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Posted by Nicholas Hastings \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Japan is no longer the big bad wolf that will blow the yen down.\par \par \uc2 First of all, the wolf doesn't appear so keen to start blowing again as the dollar falls to a new 15-month low of Y81.14. And second, even if it does start blowing, the yen won't fall for long. A lot seems to have changed from a month ago, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  launched the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's largest ever market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . As the dollar slipped under Y83 for the first time in 15 years, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent Y2 trillion pushing it back up close to Y86.\par \par \uc2 Since then, despite repeated warnings of more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar has slipped back even further, to trade well under Y82.\par \par \uc2 The latest decline has taken place amidst a flurry of international attention on the so-called "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war" issue, in which many other countries have also been trying to push their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  down against a weak dollar. Although there has been little overt criticism of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , given the continued weakness of the Japanese economy, and Japanese politicians have continued to warn that they reserve the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again, there is a growing impression that the government may be seeking other methods to lower the yen's appeal to buyers.\par \par \uc2 Among these is further monetary easing, with Bank of Japan Governor Misaaki Shirakawa pointing out that the bank could expand its recently announced Y5 trillion asset purchase program if needed. In the meantime, Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda has been criticizing other countries that have resorted to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In the case of South Korea, he warned that the country's position as chair of the G-20, which will be holding its summit of leaders in Seoul next month, "will be seriously questioned." Of course, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is probably reluctant to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again largely because of the risk that another \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  failure would pose to the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's credibility.\par \par \uc2 Also, since September, speculation over more quantitative easing in the U.S. has intensified, putting further downward pressure on the dollar against the yen. But it is just this lack of resolve on the part of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  that could make matters worse for the yen. With the dollar now trading so far below Y83, market participants have been able to conclude that the Bank of Japan isn't prepared to defend that level as originally thought.\par \par \uc2 On the contrary, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's, or for that matter the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's, policy now appears to be one of just slowing the yen's rise rather than stopping it.\par \par \uc2 As recent studies have shown, at least some of the support for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is coming from irreversible flows from basic commercial demand as well as Chinese interest in Japanese government bonds as Beijing seeks to find a home for the country's swelling \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.\par \par \uc2 New data Wednesday showed that the reserves had risen by another $194 billion in the third quarter alone to $2.65 trillion as the country continues to resist any acceleration in the appreciation of the yuan against the dollar. If that is the case, then market participants will be much less concerned about buying the yen, even if there is further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as they can be that much more confident that the yen will still spring back.\par \par \uc2 So what looked like the big bad wolf only a month ago, is now looking like more like a harmless teddy bear.\par \par \uc2 -For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 14, 2010 05:27 ET (09:27 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020101014e6ae001rw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
