\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks Edge Down; Foreclosure Concerns Drag On Financials\b0\par\par\uc2 2,574 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Oktober 2010\par 00:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      11094.57   -1.51  -0.01% \par Nasdaq     2435.38   -5.85  -0.24% \par S&P 500    1173.81   -4.29  -0.36% \par FTSE 100   5727.21  -20.14  -0.35% \par Xetra DAX  6455.27  +20.75  +0.32% \par  \par 2Y Tsy  99 31/32  -1/32 0.383% +1.6BP \par 5Y Tsy 100 12/32  -8/32 1.174% +5.5BP \par 10Y Tsy 101 3/32 -21/32 2.497% +7.5BP \par  \par USD/JPY       81.44-48     -0.43% \par Range         81.84-80.90 \par EUR/USD       1.4082-89    +0.89% \par Range         1.4121-1.3956 \par AUD/USD       0.9941-46    +0.36% \par Range         0.9993-0.9897 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol    11.90-12.20% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol    13.34-13.66% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol    14.10-14.40% \par  \par Comex Gold          $1,377.60 +$7.10 \par Comex Gold Range    $1,371.10-$1,388.10 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $82.69   -$0.32 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks closed modestly lower Thursday as investors retreated from financials because of concerns over banks' foreclosure practices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average fell 1.51 points, or 0.01%, to 11094.57, snapping a four-session winning streak in a day of choppy trading.\par \par \uc2 Financials led the Dow's declines. \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  dropped 69 cents, or 5.2%, to 12.60 and \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase\uc2  fell $1.12, or 2.8%, to $38.72. Both are among the wave of banks reviewing their foreclosures after temporarily suspending evictions due to concerns over "robo signers," who approve hundreds of foreclosure documents a day. Among other banks reviewing foreclosures, \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  fell 5.1%.\par \par \uc2 The drop follows probes by the 50 states' attorneys general into allegations that thousands of home foreclosures were improperly handled. The flood of mortgage worries could distract from potentially positive third-quarter earnings reports expected in the next several weeks, analysts said. \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  shares fell 19 cents, or 4.5%, to 4.06.\par \par \uc2 "The big banks that were most involved in not only originating mortgages but the securitization of mortgages are the ones that are most at risk here," said Don Wordell, portfolio manager of the RidgeWorth Mid-Cap Value Fund. But regional banks likely don't deserve the hit their shares have also taken, he said. "More often than not, they sold the mortgages to an agency or kept them on their balance sheets," he said.\par \par \uc2 Huntington Bancshares\uc2  shed 16 cents, or 2.6%, to 5.72, while \uc2 Fifth Third Bancorp\uc2  fell 36 cents, or 2.8%, to 12.34 and \uc2 Zions Bancorp\uc2  lost 46 cents, or 2.1%, to 21.36.\par \par \uc2 But investors also said the market may also just be taking a natural breather after hitting its highest close since early May on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "The bigger picture is that we had a great rally in September and into October," said Jonathan Vyorst, senior vice president and portfolio manager at Paradigm Capital Management.\par \par \uc2 The technology-oriented Nasdaq Composite shed 5.85 points, or 0.24%, to 2435.38. The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index fell 4.29, or 0.36%, to 1173.81, weighed by the financial sector's 1.8% decline.\par \par \uc2 Yahoo\uc2  rose 68 cents, or 4.5%, to 15.93, after The Wall Street Journal reported AOL and several private-equity firms, including \uc2 Silver Lake Partners\uc2  and \uc2 Blackstone Group\uc2 , are considering a possible takeover bid for the Internet company.\par \par \uc2 U.S. jobs data added to jitters Thursday. The number of U.S. workers filing new claims for jobless benefits increased more than expected last week. The U.S. trade deficit also rose in August, inflated by a record-setting deficit with its largest trading partner, China.\par \par \uc2 China's shares ended at their highest level in more than five months Thursday, led by banks after a newspaper reported an official inspection had found CNY2 trillion worth of loans to local governments are at high risk of default, largely in line with expectations.\par \par \uc2 "Bank stocks have already pre-digested the risks from bad loans to local governments," said Zhang Zhuo, an analyst at Minsheng Securities. "Now that the report has lent definition to the exact extent of these risks, the worst appears to be behind us," said Zhang Zhuo, an analyst at Minsheng Securities.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the Shanghai index will likely test resistance at 3000 later this month because of ample liquidity.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks rose sharply Thursday with higher commodity prices lifting resource stocks while bellwether exporters such as \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  rallied as investors temporarily brushed aside concerns about the strong yen on hopes for bold U.S. easing measures to prop up the global economy.\par \par \uc2 "There seems to be a temperature difference between the impact of a stronger yen on the Japanese economy as a whole and on individual exporters," said Takashi Ushio, general manager at \uc2 Marusan Securities\uc2 , noting increased efforts by Japanese firms to offset \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  impact on their earnings.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong shares ended at another 28-month high Thursday, with market volume jumping to the highest level in more than 17 months as investors bought heavily into mainland Chinese lenders and financial firms, which have lagged the broad market's earlier gains.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the city's benchmark index will likely rise to around 25,000-26,000 points by the end of this year, driven by abundant liquidity because of U.S. dollar weakness and near-zero interest rates, though they expect the blue-chip index to face resistance at 24,000 points in the near term amid a lack of fresh leads to spur buying interest.\par \par \uc2 Crude oil futures retreated from early gains Thursday in U.S. trading, settling lower as a government report showed U.S. demand for petroleum products fell to the lowest level in nearly a year.\par \par \uc2 Gold futures printed a fresh record settlement Thursday as monetary tightening in Singapore sent investors out of the U.S. dollar and into the perceived hedge of the metal.\par \par \uc2 Base metals closed mixed on the London Metal Exchange Thursday after toppling from multi-month highs in jittery trade as U.S. labor figures disappointed.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar fell against a broad range of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Thursday as prospects for Asian economic growth contrasted with the likely need for more stimulus in the U.S.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A monetary-tightening move by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore accelerated the dollar's slide, knocking the greenback to long-term lows against rivals around the globe before the dollar regained some poise in New York trading.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors viewed the MAS decision to widen and raise the Singapore dollar's trading band as an indication Asian economies are now strong enough to tolerate monetary tightening, while the dollar languishes under the likelihood of more easing by the Federal Reserve.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This continues a theme of what we have already seen," said Win Thin, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brown Brothers Harriman\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York. There is very little reason to hold dollars as an investor now, and with Federal Reserve stimulus largely priced in, Singapore's move gave investors another reason to sell dollars, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors will be listening closely Friday to Fed Chairman Ben Bernanke's speech at a conference in Boston for clues on the timing and extent of any further quantitative easing measures. Any hints that asset-purchase programs might not be as extensive as once thought would support the greenback, analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Singapore news accelerated dollar selling that had already been stoked by concern over low U.S. interest rates, a run-up in Asian equities this week and large gains in commodities like gold and oil. The combination drove the yen as well as higher-yielding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to notable levels overnight.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar ground down to a fresh 15-year-low against the yen of Y80.88, with investors on high alert for yen-weakening \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The ICE Dollar Index dropped to its lowest level this year at 76.259. China's yuan surged to its highest point against the dollar since the Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  began regular trading in 1994. The South African rand rose to its best level since January 2008, while the Turkish lira touched a two-year peak.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "A broader emerging market move against the dollar is starting to become more acceptable," said Steven Englander, head of G10 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro broke to $1.4123, its highest level since January. The Canadian dollar traded below parity for the first time since April, while the Australian dollar hit US$.9993, its highest point since it was floated in 1983. The dollar dropped to an all-time low against the Swiss franc at CHF0.9464.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In New York trading, the dollar recovered from its worst levels, but remained sharply lower on the day. The Canadian dollar moved slightly away from parity, with the U.S. dollar trading at C$1.0031 from C$1.0038 late Wednesday. It reached a low of C$0.9976 in earlier trading.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I think people were just a little worried about being overstretched at this point," said Thin.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Thursday afternoon in New York, the euro was at $1.4077 from $1.3964 from late Wednesday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y81.51 from Y81.74, while the euro was at Y114.74 from Y114.14. The U.K. pound was at $1.6013 from $1.5892. The dollar was at CHF0.9527 from CHF0.9584. The Dollar Index was at 76.563 from 77.060.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With the dollar perilously close to its all-time low against the yen, investors are keenly attuned to any reaction from Japanese authorities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Prime Minster Naoto Kan said Thursday that excess volatility in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates is unfavorable, Kyodo News reported. The comment came after the dollar fell briefly to Y80.88 for the first time since April 1995.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A tepid 30-year bond sale fueled broad selling in the Treasury market Thursday, sending the benchmark 10-year note's yield to the highest level since the start of the month.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The auction erased an earlier rally in the 30-year bond and turned it into the biggest loser in the market. The yield hit the highest level since Sept. 20. [ 14-10-10 2205GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The $13 billion auction garnered the smallest demand since February, wrapping up this week's $66 billion new government notes and bonds supply.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All three sales for the week, including $32 billion in three-year notes Tuesday and $21 billion in 10-year notes Wednesday, were weaker than recent average, sending signals that ultralow bond yields have started to push many investors to seek higher returns in other assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bond yields have plunged in recent weeks as speculation grew that the Federal Reserve will step up government-debt purchases to support the economy, known as quantitative easing, when it meets to set monetary policy in early November.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  said the rally has seen its peak, while bond powerhouse \f28 \uc2 \f2 Pacific Investment Management Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . has sought better returns in high-grade corporate bonds and mortgage-backed securities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Buyers finally said 'enough' to a series of auctions at or near record yields and refused to chase prices further," said George Goncalves, head of U.S. interest rates strategy at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nomura Securities International\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of 1955 GMT, the benchmark 10-year note was 21/32 lower to yield 2.497% and the 30-year bond was 1 16/32 lower to yield 3.903%. Bond prices move inversely to yields.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 10-year note's yield touched 2.515%, the highest level since Oct. 1. The 30-year bond's yield hit 3.916%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds ended lower Thursday as funds were diverted to the local share market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Asset management firms and insurers are increasing equity investment while cutting bond holdings in recent days," a Shanghai-based trader with a local broker said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The PBOC's move to inject a net CNY181 billion of funds into the banking system via regular open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this week suggests it's unwilling to allow market rates to rise sharply though.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Short-term support for the Shanghai exchange government bond index is tipped at 126.5.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government bond futures ended slightly higher Thursday, but gains were limited as better-than-expected 30-year tender results and rising Tokyo share prices gave investors mixed cues.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The firm auction suggests life insurers may have lowered their target yields amid ultra-easy financial conditions and that banks, which are usually active in the medium-term sector, may have become more interested in longer-maturity bonds due to unattractive yields in the shorter end, said Katsutoshi Inadome, a strategist at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But despite the tender results, JGBs are expected to be capped in the near term as "players will likely remain cautious about a 20-year JGB auction coming up next Thursday," said Maki Shimizu, a senior strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  Global Markets Japan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The benchmark 10-year cash JGB yield is likely to trade in a 0.86%-0.90% range over the next week, Shimizu said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par US Trade Deficit Jumps, Jobless Claims Rise \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Google\f28 \uc2 \f2  Profit Up 32%; Results Top Views \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Yahoo\f28 \uc2 \f2  Shares Up On AOL, Private Equity Rumors \par AMD 3Q Loss Narrows On Higher Sales \par Fed Survey Shows Credit Flowing More Freely \par CME Interest-rate Swap Clearing Seen Oct. 18 \par CVS To Pay $75M Fine, Forfeit Meth Profit \par Consumer Loan-backed Bonds Remain Sluggish \par NJ Agency Sells $1.5B Muni Bonds At Juicy Yields \par CMBS Delinquencies Rise By Least Amount In 2 Years \par Rosengren Says Buying Treasurys One Option \par Japan PM Kan: Excess Volatility In \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Market Unfavorable -Kyodo \par Chinese Credit Growth Could Be Storing Up Trouble Warns Fitch \par =China Mobile-Tech Co Trunkbow Plans $125.5M IPO Of Common Stock \par WSJ: U.S. Sales Rate In Oct May Be Highest Of 2010, Toyota Exec Says \par =US Lines Up New Aviation Deals With Japan, Israel