\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Swoons Vs Broad Range Of  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrew J. Johnson and Robert Flint   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 828 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2010\par 22:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar fell against a broad range of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday as prospects for Asian economic growth contrasted with the likely need for more stimulus in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 A monetary-tightening move by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore accelerated the dollar's slide, knocking the greenback to long-term lows against rivals around the globe before the dollar regained some poise in New York trading.\par \par \uc2 Investors viewed the MAS decision to widen and raise the Singapore dollar's trading band as an indication Asian economies are now strong enough to tolerate monetary tightening, while the dollar languishes under the likelihood of more easing by the Federal Reserve.\par \par \uc2 "This continues a theme of what we have already seen," said Win Thin, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  in New York. There is very little reason to hold dollars as an investor now, and with Federal Reserve stimulus largely priced in, Singapore's move gave investors another reason to sell dollars, he said.\par \par \uc2 Investors will be listening closely Friday to Fed Chairman Ben Bernanke's speech at a conference in Boston for clues on the timing and extent of any further quantitative easing measures. Any hints that asset-purchase programs might not be as extensive as once thought would support the greenback, said analysts.\par \par \uc2 The Singapore news accelerated dollar selling that had already been stoked by concern over low U.S. interest rates, a run-up in Asian equities this week and large gains in commodities like gold and oil. The combination drove the yen as well as higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to notable levels overnight.\par \par \uc2 The dollar ground down to a fresh 15-year-low against the yen of Y80.88, with investors on high alert for yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The ICE Dollar Index dropped to its lowest level this year at 76.259. China's yuan surged to its highest point against the dollar since the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  began regular trading in 1994. The South African rand rose to its best level since January 2008, while the Turkish lira touched a two-year peak.\par \par \uc2 "A broader emerging market move against the dollar is starting to become more acceptable," said Steven Englander, head of G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The euro broke to $1.4123, its highest level since January. The Canadian dollar traded below parity for the first time since April, while the Australian dollar hit US$.9993, its highest point since it was floated in 1983. The dollar dropped to an all-time low against the Swiss franc at CHF0.9464.\par \par \uc2 In New York trading, the dollar recovered from its worst levels, but remained sharply lower on the day. The Canadian dollar moved slightly away from parity, with the U.S. dollar trading at C$1.0031 from C$1.0038 late Wednesday. It reached a low of C$0.9976 in earlier trading.\par \par \uc2 "I think people were just a little worried about being overstretched at this point," said Thin.\par \par \uc2 Late Thursday afternoon, the euro was at $1.4077 from $1.3964 from late Wednesday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y81.51 from Y81.74, while the euro was at Y114.74 from Y114.14. The U.K. pound was at $1.6013 from $1.5892. The dollar was at CHF0.9527 from CHF0.9584. The Dollar Index was at 76.563 from 77.060.\par \par \uc2 To see the euro's performance against the dollar, please see:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4756"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4756}}}\par \par \uc2 To see the Australian dollar's performance against the U.S. dollar, please see:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4757"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4757}}}\par \par \uc2 With the dollar perilously close to its all-time low against the yen, investors are keenly attuned to any reaction from Japanese authorities.\par \par \uc2 Japanese Prime Minster Naoto Kan said Thursday that excess volatility in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates is unfavorable, Kyodo News reported. The comment came after the dollar fell briefly to Y80.88 for the first time since April 1995.\par \par \uc2 Different trading systems report different levels as the all-time low in the dollar against the yen, but Y79.75 is widely viewed as the benchmark figure for the all-time low. Interbank trading system EBS, which is often used as a source of market rates, puts the all-time low slightly higher at Y79.92.\par \par \uc2 With the ICE Dollar Index declining, \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 's PowerShares U.S. Dollar Index Bearish exchange-traded fund was up 0.56% from late Wednesday, while its PowerShares U.S. Dollar Index Bullish was down 0.67%. The two exchange-traded funds are based on \uc2 Deutsche Bank\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -futures indexes, whose composition mirrors that of the ICE's Dollar Index.\par \par \uc2 -By Andrew J. Johnson, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-3092; andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com [ 10-14-10 1626ET ]\par \par \uc2 1033 1074 1158 1863 1865 20714 28038 4164   \par \par \uc2 Dokume