\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Not Blowing The JPY House Down\b0\par\par\uc2 1,430 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2010\par 09:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0715 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan is no longer the big bad wolf that will blow the JPY down, according to Nicholas Hastings in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Focus. First of all, the wolf doesn't appear so keen to start blowing again as the USD falls to a new 15-month low of Y81.14. And second, even if it does start blowing, the JPY won't fall for long. A lot seems to have changed from a month ago, when the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  launched the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's largest ever market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0713 GMT [Dow Jones] The DMO auctions GBP3.75 B UKT 2015 Thursday and \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  expects good demand at the sale. "With banks still building their liquidity buffers and the risk of an extension of the asset purchase facility as early as this year, we expect a decent auction," \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  says. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0709 GMT [Dow Jones] China's cabbage market is anything but sour. Cabbage prices surging since South Korea, looking to soothe shortage there, dropped tariffs on cabbage imports. Prices for cabbage in past few days +12% to CNY5-CNY6/kg, according to Chinese media site Sina. While urban Chinese can access variety of produce, can afford to pay for more expensive vegetables, rural majority has far less flexibility. Higher costs for cabbage, which many in rural regions rely on for cheap nutrition source in winter, tough to swallow. Certainly, some Chinese farmers will likely prosper. Bad weather sharply reduced cabbage crop, with some Koreans paying $10-$14/head. But China's economic planners aren't eager to import Korea's inflation after food-pricing mania wreaked havoc during 2007 pork shortage, caused by disease outbreak; resulted in year-long rise of grocery bills, helped push inflation to 11-year-high of 6.9% in November 2008. Pork prices and China's inflation rate have since fallen, government seeks to avoid another economic pickle. See: =WSJ BLOG/China Real Time: Korea's Kimchi Crisis Comes to China (laurie.burkitt@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0708 GMT [Dow Jones] The dollar slumped across the board in Asia Thursday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore sprung a surprise by increasing the Singapore dollar's trading band against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and also widened its policy band, both albeit 'slightly'. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been expected to stay on hold, thus caught the markets by surprise and they reacted by a wholesale selloff of the greenback. USD/SGD sank to a record low of 1.2893, USD/JPY a fresh 15 year low of 81.14, USD/CNY fixed at another post-revaluation low of 6.6582, USD/CAD breached parity, and AUD/USD almost made it there, printing a fresh post float high of 0.9986. In the majors, EUR/USD added one cent to a fresh eight month high of 1.4098, GBP/USD broke back above 1.60, and USD/CHF hit another record low of 0.9480, and gold made a fresh all-time high of $1,386.70 a troy ounce. Away from this carnage Fed hawk Lacker said late Wednesday that he didn't support more QE, while BOE hawk Sentance reiterated the need for higher UK interest rates. For Thursday there is no European data on tap, while in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  it's difficult to see what will stand in the way of more dollar selling. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0708 GMT [Dow Jones] The zloty is at its strongest level against the euro since April, after breaking through key levels overnight as unexpectedly high CPI for September, at 2.5%, strengthens expectations for the start of monetary policy tightening, a trader says. EUR/PLN is at 3.8916 from 3.9011 late Wednesday, USD/PLN at 2.7617 from 2.7914. (marcin.sobczyk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0707 GMT [Dow Jones] Apparently unworried by the strength of the euro, falling global demand and tough fiscal tightening measures, the ECB appears confident that further liquidity easing in the form of QE or repo \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not be necessary, unlike the dovish tones coming from the Fed and the BOE. But, Capital Economics believes the euro zone risks falling back into recession. "Fiscal tightening has already begun to hit the peripheral euro-zone economies and will soon spread to the core," it says. "This, together with ongoing market concerns, will keep fears of a break-up alive." (nick.andrews@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0706 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia shares turn lower, KLCI down 0.3% at 1,493.01, off intraday high of 1,503.82. Construction, plantation stocks continue to rise, but profit-taking in some blue-chips keeping index under pressure. Dealer says, "Some selling pressure is bound to be there beyond the 1,500 level, but with both international and local cues positive, KLCI will likely continue to rise in the short term." Adds, with strong CPO price outlook, plantation stocks likely to continue to support index. May find support at psychological 1,490 level. Market breadth also turning negative with 368 gainers vs 373 decliners. Among gainers, AirAsia (5099.KU) +4.6% at MYR2.28 on strong earnings outlook; plantation gainers include \uc2 KL Kepong\uc2  (2445.KU) +3.0% at MYR19.16, \uc2 IOI Corp\uc2 . (1961.KU) +1.0% at MYR5.80. Among decliners, Unisem (M) (5005.KU) down 2.9% at MYR1.98, \uc2 Axiata Group\uc2  (6888.KU) down 1.1% at MYR4.54. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0704 GMT [Dow Jones] There is likely to be good demand for Italian BTP 4% February 2037 bond at auction Thursday--as part of an up to EUR5.5 billion auction in three BTP lines--due to its scarcity, says \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 . "There won't be many other opportunities to go long 30-year BTP in 2010, and that's why we think there will be good demand for this paper," it says. \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  favors BTPs in general into the fourth quarter as Italy has already completed almost 90% of its expected 2010 issuance, it says. Results are due at 0900 GMT. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0658 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CHF looks bearish and should it break below 0.9500 has downside potential to 0.9225, according to \uc2 UBS\uc2 . On the upside, there is strong technical resistance at 0.9729, with limited potential thereafter to 0.9918, says analyst Gareth Berry. USD/CHF at 0.9510 by 0656 GMT. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0657 GMT [Dow Jones] A super-charged euro is again pushing the rand stronger against the dollar, adding to the consternation of local officials already concerned about the strength of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . USD/ZAR is at 6.7633 from a close of 6.8185. An analyst says 6.71 is the next target, although the focus for the day is purely on the euro-dollar pair. EUR/USD trades at 1.4094. (I-Net Bridge)\par \par \uc2 0647 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI +0.9% at 23,668.80, with intraday peak of 23,788.94 highest since June 6, 2008, tracking regional gains after U.S. stocks' rise; volume huge at HK$84.90 billion. While market very overbought with HSI's 9-day RSI above 85, abundant liquidity appears to more than offset profit-taking pressure. \uc2 South China Securities\uc2  says, "with a horde of liquidity in the market on the back of a weak U.S. dollar, anticipation on more quantitative easing to come and the 5th Plenary Session opening this Friday to set the roadmap for the 12th Five-year Plan, the short-term outlook of the HK bourse remains optimistic." HKEx (0388.HK) prime beneficiary of such strong liquidity, +3.2% at HK$175.30, but best-performing blue chip overall ICBC (1398.HK), +3.3% at HK$6.25 after recent underperformance due to \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 ' stake cut. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0643 GMT [Dow Jones] Steeper levels on the UST 10s/30s yield curve are set to be tested Thursday as the Treasury auctions $13B 30-year USTs. The 10s/30s curve has steepened 13.4 bps over the last week, says Ajay Rajadhyaksha, \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  strategist. "Almost the entire steepening can be explained by the level of intermediate yields and breakevens," Rajadhyaksha says. The 10s/30s spread currently trades at 138 bps. (nick.andrews@dowjones.com) [ 10-14-10 0315ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101014e6ae0005e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
