\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Australia's Lonely  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  - HEARD ON THE ST\b0\par\par\uc2 1,742 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2010\par 06:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0457 GMT [Dow Jones] As many other developed economies consider further easing of monetary policy, Australia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stands alone in contemplating further rate rises, says Heard on the Street; since RBA left rates on hold last week, surprising many, strong consumer sentiment and jobs data has made a hike next month seem a certainty. But AUD approaching parity with USD, a worry for Australia's exporting industries; already there's talk success of the country's mining sector, on the back of Chinese growth, is causing "Dutch disease" -- the concern being that success of resources industry could dampen prospects for rest of economy. See: HEARD ON THE STREET: Australia's Lonely \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (andrew.peaple@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0446 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares end +0.2% at 458.66 as foreigners extend their recent buying-streak amid growing optimism more banks will lower lending rates, while government continues to help companies lower \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  costs. "Market sentiment will improve because more banks are expected to cut their lending rates. Also, foreigners are buying these days so we expect more gains tomorrow," says Eurocapital Securities analyst. Volume thin with 23.3 million shares valued at VND597 billion traded. Among actives, Meco (MCG.VH) ends +4.7% at VND17,900, \uc2 PetroVietnam\uc2  Fertilizer (DPM.VH) ends +1.8% at VND33,500, Ocean Group (OGC.VH) ends down 0.3% at VND30,000. Tips key index to move in 460-465 Friday. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0444 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia shares turn flat after breaching psychological 1,500 level; profit-taking in blue-chips erase early gains; KLCI up 0.35 points at 1,497.32 midday. Dealer says short term positive trend intact, but adds 1,500 to act as strong barrier with profit-taking likely to limit gains at any rally; tips 1,495-1,505 range for rest of session. Investors continue to favor construction stocks on hopes of positive newsflow from 2011 budget tomorrow; sub-index +0.7%; Market breadth slightly positive with 363 gainers vs 334 decliners; gainers include plantation stocks \uc2 KL Kepong\uc2  (2445.KU) +3.9% at MYR19.32, \uc2 IOI Corp\uc2 . (1961.KU) +1.0% at MYR5.80; \uc2 MMC Corp\uc2 . (2194.KU) +1.3% at MYR3.10; Manulife (1058.KU) +12% at MYR3.18 after major shareholder makes offer to buy out rest of shares in insurer; among decliners \uc2 GuocoLand\uc2  (1503.KU) down 5.0% at MYR1.15, MK Land (8893.KU) down 2.8% at MYR0.345, both after yesterday's gains.(ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0440 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD parity seems inevitable, but concerns growing a pullback could be in cards if central banks don't do what market expects them to, says Sydney-based \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist. "Fundamentally, there is little standing in the way and it's pretty much at parity already. But there remains this possibility the RBA won't go (to higher rates) in November on the threat of commercial banks raising lending rates. And the FOMC could decide to implement other measures before they buy Treasury securities outright," says the strategist. AUD/USD recently at 0.9960, just off new post-float high of 0.9982 hit in early afternoon trading. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0440 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares pare gains as investors take some money off table with prices at 29-month highs. Market breadth flat vs 5 gainers for every decliner in early trade. STI +0.1% at 3206.83 midday vs 3220.51 morning high (new year-to-date intraday peak, highest since May 2008), which could provide resistance for rest of day. "The market has come off a bit, with some mixed reaction to the MAS tightening. But a pullback shouldn't be surprising as we had a good run yesterday," says local house trader. Wilmar (F34.SG) top percentage gainer among blue chips, +4.1% at S$6.40, on hopes plantation group will benefit from higher cooking oil prices in China. \uc2 Keppel Corp\uc2 . (BN4.SG) down 0.7% at S$9.78, SembMarine (S51.SG) off 0.2% at S$4.40; stronger SGD vs USD deemed tad negative for rig builders as bulk of their contracts denominated in USD. Overall volume modest at 1.19 billion shares worth S$1.17 billion. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0437 GMT [Dow Jones] After downbeat production report from its 75%-owned \uc2 Coal & Allied\uc2  (CNA.AU) yesterday, \uc2 UBS\uc2  expected production declines and guidance downgrades for \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2 's (RIO.AU) coal production. Thermal coal FY10 guidance came in worse than \uc2 UBS\uc2  estimate, 19.1 million tons vs 19.7 million tons; but most valuable hard coking coal was guided at 9.5 million tons in FY10 vs 9.4 million tons estimate, while 2.4 million tons mined in 3Q came in well above 2.2 million tons estimate. Copper more downbeat, coming in at 159,000 tons mined vs 169,900 estimate. (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0436 GMT [Dow Jones] Volatilities implied by 1-month ATM USD/JPY options down tad at 11.60%/12.30% from 11.65%/12.35% Wednesday in New York as stability in spot early in morning in global day prompted some players to trim USD downside hedges, says options dealer at major Japan bank. But sharp falls in vols unlikely for rest of global day as some demand for USD downside hedges later returns after USD/JPY marks fresh 15-year low at 81.23 in late Tokyo morning trade. Such demand may be sustained ahead amid view Japan PM Kan's comments Wednesday that G-20 countries, including South Korea, China, need to "act responsibly" in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, might have made it difficult for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market again. Says earlier in day, before spot fell below 81.50, multiple names bought overnight USD-put/JPY-call options contract with strike price of 81.50 with implied vols of 13.75%, in unknown amounts.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0436 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI +1.1% at 23,704.70 midday, earlier touching 23,788.94, highest intraday level since June 6, 2008, bolstered by gains in regional markets on Wall Street cue. With index rising for 8th session out of 9, near-term profit-taking likely, but more long-term upside expected. \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  raises HSI's end-2011 target to 26,500 from 23,050, to "reflect improved earnings expectations and our revised target multiple of 14.5X (from the historical average of 12.8X)." HKEx (0388.HK) rallies 3.5% to HK$175.80 on sharp increase in HK market volume, which hits HK$78.09 billion at interval; full-day volume may approach HK$150 billion mark, last time HK market had over HK$150 billion volume was January 2008. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0434 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 0240 GMT did not mention the source of a loan inspection. The correct version follows:) China shares extend gains, led by banks after analysts say nothing new indicated in a report in state-run China Securities Journal which said Chinese officials' inspection of loans to local-government financing platforms found CNY2 trillion worth at high risk of default. Shanghai Composite Index +1.7% at 2910.62, faces immediate resistance at 2950. "Banks are leading because they already pre-digested risks from bad loans to local governments. Today after the report came out, the loan risks are more certain and there appears to be no other risks to worry about," says Zhang Zhuo at Minsheng Securities. \uc2 China Merchants Bank\uc2  (600036.SH) +3.7% at CNY15.02, Bank of China (601988.SH) +2.3% at CNY3.53. Shenzhen Composite Index flat at 1229.48. (esther.fung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0426 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares end higher, buoyed by continuing investor confidence following overnight advance of U.S. equities. Main gauge PSEi +0.9% to 4233.38 amid heavy trading. "Foreign investors are banking on strong management from the government and robust expectations for the country's economy," says Emmanuel Soller, Equity World trader. Tips market may head towards 4280 resistance Friday as market eyes overseas cues; next support 4200. Leading market advance are property developer Megaworld (MEG.PH) +6.9% at PHP2.49; PLDT (TEL.PH) +0.6% at PHP2,708' SM Investments (SM.PH), +3.9% at PHP535. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0424 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY extends fall to fresh intraday low under current system midday on broad USD weakness in Asia; on OTC, USD/CNY at 6.6546 vs 6.6641 last close; 6.6500 may support. "The record low central parity rate today was driven by the weak dollar index," says Shanghai-based trader at European bank. PBOC sets fixing at record low 6.6582 vs 6.6693 yesterday. "But trading range in the morning hovers around the parity rate level as people aren't sure how the customer flows may drive the market later in the session." Offshore, 1-year USD/CNY at 6.4380/6.4440 vs 6.4580/6.4630 late yesterday. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0418 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei +2.0% at 9593.83, staying firm even after USD/JPY sinks to fresh 15-year low (now 81.25). "The market is being supported by expectations for more liquidity on the back of anticipated large-scale U.S. monetary easing and following Japan's drastic easing steps," says \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2  strategist Hideyuki Ishiguro. Adds foreign investors' risk tolerance is increasing. Pegs near-term Nikkei resistance at 9700 on assumption \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Support seen at 9400 if USD/JPY doesn't fall below Y80. All 33 Topix subindexes higher. Real estate sector best performer (+4.1%) following robust Tokyo area September new condo sales data. \uc2 Mitsui Fudosan\uc2  (8801.TO) up 4.3% to Y1,620 and Mitsubishi Estate (8802.TO) up 4.8% to Y1,537. Dentsu (4324.TO) up 5.6% at Y1,970 on \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  rating hike. \uc2 Shinsei Bank\uc2  (8303.TO) off 5.3% at Y71 on profit-taking following recent sharp gains. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com) [ 10-14-10 0057ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101014e6ae0003z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
