 \highlight\uc2  market again. Says earlier in day, before spot fell below 81.50, multiple names bought overnight USD-put/JPY-call options contract with strike price of 81.50 with implied vols of 13.75%, in unknown amounts.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0436 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI +1.1% at 23,704.70 midday, earlier touching 23,788.94, highest intraday level since June 6, 2008, bolstered by gains in regional markets on Wall Street cue. With index rising for 8th session out of 9, near-term profit-taking likely, but more long-term upside expected. \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  raises HSI's end-2011 target to 26,500 from 23,050, to "reflect improved earnings expectations and our revised target multiple of 14.5X (from the historical average of 12.8X)." HKEx (0388.HK) rallies 3.5% to HK$175.80 on sharp increase in HK market volume, which hits HK$78.09 billion at interval; full-day volume may approach HK$150 billion mark, last time HK market had over HK$150 billion volume was January 2008. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0434 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 0240 GMT did not mention the source of a loan inspection. The correct version follows:) China shares extend gains, led by banks after analysts say nothing new indicated in a report in state-run China Securities Journal which said Chinese officials' inspection of loans to local-government financing platforms found CNY2 trillion worth at high risk of default. Shanghai Composite Index +1.7% at 2910.62, faces immediate resistance at 2950. "Banks are leading because they already pre-digested risks from bad loans to local governments. Today after the report came out, the loan risks are more certain and there appears to be no other risks to worry about," says Zhang Zhuo at Minsheng Securities. \uc2 China Merchants Bank\uc2  (600036.SH) +3.7% at CNY15.02, Bank of China (601988.SH) +2.3% at CNY3.53. Shenzhen Composite Index flat at 1229.48. (esther.fung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0426 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares end higher, buoyed by continuing investor confidence following overnight advance of U.S. equities. Main gauge PSEi +0.9% to 4233.38 amid heavy trading. "Foreign investors are banking on strong management from the government and robust expectations for the country's economy," says Emmanuel Soller, Equity World trader. Tips market may head towards 4280 resistance Friday as market eyes overseas cues; next support 4200. Leading market advance are property developer Megaworld (MEG.PH) +6.9% at PHP2.49; PLDT (TEL.PH) +0.6% at PHP2,708' SM Investments (SM.PH), +3.9% at PHP535. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0424 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY extends fall to fresh intraday low under current system midday on broad USD weakness in Asia; on OTC, USD/CNY at 6.6546 vs 6.6641 last close; 6.6500 may support. "The record low central parity rate today was driven by the weak dollar index," says Shanghai-based trader at European bank. PBOC sets fixing at record low 6.6582 vs 6.6693 yesterday. "But trading range in the morning hovers around the parity rate level as people aren't sure how the customer flows may drive the market later in the session." Offshore, 1-year USD/CNY at 6.4380/6.4440 vs 6.4580/6.4630 late yesterday. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0418 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei +2.0% at 9593.83, staying firm even after USD/JPY sinks to fresh 15-year low (now 81.25). "The market is being supported by expectations for more liquidity on the back of anticipated large-scale U.S. monetary easing and following Japan's drastic easing steps," says \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2  strategist Hideyuki Ishiguro. Adds foreign investors' risk tolerance is increasing. Pegs near-term Nikkei resistance at 9700 on assumption \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Support seen at 9400 if USD/JPY doesn't fall below Y80. All 33 Topix subindexes higher. Real estate sector best performer (+4.1%) following robust Tokyo area September new condo sales data. \uc2 Mitsui Fudosan\uc2  (8801.TO) up 4.3% to Y1,620 and Mitsubishi Estate (8802.TO) up 4.8% to Y1,537. Dentsu (4324.TO) up 5.6% at Y1,970 on \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  rating hike. \uc2 Shinsei Bank\uc2  (8303.TO) off 5.3% at Y71 on profit-taking following recent sharp gains. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0417 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  maintains cautiously optimistic outlook for Hong Kong economy after Wednesday Policy Address. Says yet to be seen whether latest policy initiatives (includes boosting land supply) will help alleviate challenges in housing. But "Hong Kong has had to cope with considerable volatility in monetary conditions and asset markets, which has resulted in undesirably large fluctuations in economic activity, or boom-bust economic cycles," says house. "The recent gains in asset (equity as well as property) markets amidst renewed expectations of further quantitative easing from developed markets has further heightened our concerns for excess volatility and instability ahead when conditions reverse." Adds, expansion of offshore CNY financial services in Hong Kong represents significant opportunities for economy in medium term, increasing circulation of CNY could complicate monetary management in Hong Kong, with considerable implications on asset markets. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0409 GMT [Dow Jones]India government bonds tad higher as Bank of Korea's decision to keep its policy rate steady raises hopes RBI will think twice before conducting any future rate hikes as concerns over pace of global economic recovery remains, says trader with state-run bank; 7.80% 2020 bond at 98.65 vs 98.58 last close. But inflation remains key driver of rate expectations and cue from weekly price print later in day could hurt market sentiment ahead of monthly WPI due Friday. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0408 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CAD touches parity on continued broad-based USD weakness amid expectations for further quantitative easing by Fed, says Nobuaki Kubo, senior dealer at \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  Investment Services. Adding to those expectations, USD also weighed by \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's surprise decision to tighten its monetary policy, which contrasts sharply with mounting expectations for U.S. Federal Reserve to soon conduct more monetary easing. Adds, broad gains in Asian share markets also prompting investors to increase bets in commodity-linked \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as CAD, as gold hits new record high. USD/CAD last 1.0005. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0407 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex up 0.4% at 20,764.19, just off 20,854.55 early high - highest level since Jan. 15, 2008, buoyed by sustained buying from institutions, positive regional markets. Gains led by software, select metal plays. \uc2 Infosys (500209.BY) \uc2 +1.9% at INR3,208.10 after +2.5% yesterday - company to announce 2Q results tomorrow. "Liquidity is chasing share prices. Software and automobile shares are seeing a fair amount of buying on expectations of strong second quarter numbers," says dealer at domestic brokerage. Adds, volume picking up. Tips Sensex in 20,660-20,880 range. Says, some profit-taking could come in later as markets now +3.0% from Tuesday's close. Wipro (507685.BY) +1.5% at INR490, \uc2 Tata Steel\uc2  (500470.BY) +2.0% at INR651. Jindal Steel (532286.BY) +1.2% at INR727.35, \uc2 Hero Honda\uc2  (500182.BY) +0.6% at INR1,889. RIL (500325.BY) +0.6% at INR1,078. But, Larsen (500510.BY) down 0.7% at INR2,064 on profit-booking. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0407 GMT [Dow Jones] Daiwa Securities Capital Markets says robust earnings released by U.S. tech companies and Japan's August machinery orders (which beat consensus expectations) should provide some support for Tokyo stocks (short-term). Nonetheless, notes the most actively traded names which were expected to lead the market up on Wednesday were mostly losers, suggesting clouded visibility for stock market's direction. In light of historical precedent, thinks Japan could benefit from sustained easy credit, in-line with monetary policy taken by U.S. Fed Chairman Bernanke (an authority on the Great Depression), and eventually overcome ongoing prolonged deflation. Adds the recent steep yen rise could cause Japan to step up efforts to counter deflationary trends, possibly providing good entry point into Tokyo stock market. USD/JPY hit fresh 15-year low today (81.17); Nikkei currently up 1.9% at 9580.13. (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com) [ 10-14-10 0037ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101014e6ae0003s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
