, USD/CNY at 6.6546 vs 6.6641 last close; 6.6500 may support. "The record low central parity rate today was driven by the weak dollar index," says Shanghai-based trader at European bank. PBOC sets fixing at record low 6.6582 vs 6.6693 yesterday. "But trading range in the morning hovers around the parity rate level as people aren't sure how the customer flows may drive the market later in the session." Offshore, 1-year USD/CNY at 6.4380/6.4440 vs 6.4580/6.4630 late yesterday. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0418 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei +2.0% at 9593.83, staying firm even after USD/JPY sinks to fresh 15-year low (now 81.25). "The market is being supported by expectations for more liquidity on the back of anticipated large-scale U.S. monetary easing and following Japan's drastic easing steps," says \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2  strategist Hideyuki Ishiguro. Adds foreign investors' risk tolerance is increasing. Pegs near-term Nikkei resistance at 9700 on assumption \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Support seen at 9400 if USD/JPY doesn't fall below Y80. All 33 Topix subindexes higher. Real estate sector best performer (+4.1%) following robust Tokyo area September new condo sales data. \uc2 Mitsui Fudosan\uc2  (8801.TO) up 4.3% to Y1,620 and Mitsubishi Estate (8802.TO) up 4.8% to Y1,537. Dentsu (4324.TO) up 5.6% at Y1,970 on \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  rating hike. \uc2 Shinsei Bank\uc2  (8303.TO) off 5.3% at Y71 on profit-taking following recent sharp gains. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0417 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  maintains cautiously optimistic outlook for Hong Kong economy after Wednesday Policy Address. Says yet to be seen whether latest policy initiatives (includes boosting land supply) will help alleviate challenges in housing. But "Hong Kong has had to cope with considerable volatility in monetary conditions and asset markets, which has resulted in undesirably large fluctuations in economic activity, or boom-bust economic cycles," says house. "The recent gains in asset (equity as well as property) markets amidst renewed expectations of further quantitative easing from developed markets has further heightened our concerns for excess volatility and instability ahead when conditions reverse." Adds, expansion of offshore CNY financial services in Hong Kong represents significant opportunities for economy in medium term, increasing circulation of CNY could complicate monetary management in Hong Kong, with considerable implications on asset markets. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0409 GMT [Dow Jones]India government bonds tad higher as Bank of Korea's decision to keep its policy rate steady raises hopes RBI will think twice before conducting any future rate hikes as concerns over pace of global economic recovery remains, says trader with state-run bank; 7.80% 2020 bond at 98.65 vs 98.58 last close. But inflation remains key driver of rate expectations and cue from weekly price print later in day could hurt market sentiment ahead of monthly WPI due Friday. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0408 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CAD touches parity on continued broad-based USD weakness amid expectations for further quantitative easing by Fed, says Nobuaki Kubo, senior dealer at \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  Investment Services. Adding to those expectations, USD also weighed by \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's surprise decision to tighten its monetary policy, which contrasts sharply with mounting expectations for U.S. Federal Reserve to soon conduct more monetary easing. Adds, broad gains in Asian share markets also prompting investors to increase bets in commodity-linked \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as CAD, as gold hits new record high. USD/CAD last 1.0005. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0407 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex up 0.4% at 20,764.19, just off 20,854.55 early high - highest level since Jan. 15, 2008, buoyed by sustained buying from institutions, positive regional markets. Gains led by software, select metal plays. \uc2 Infosys (500209.BY) \uc2 +1.9% at INR3,208.10 after +2.5% yesterday - company to announce 2Q results tomorrow. "Liquidity is chasing share prices. Software and automobile shares are seeing a fair amount of buying on expectations of strong second quarter numbers," says dealer at domestic brokerage. Adds, volume picking up. Tips Sensex in 20,660-20,880 range. Says, some profit-taking could come in later as markets now +3.0% from Tuesday's close. Wipro (507685.BY) +1.5% at INR490, \uc2 Tata Steel\uc2  (500470.BY) +2.0% at INR651. Jindal Steel (532286.BY) +1.2% at INR727.35, \uc2 Hero Honda\uc2  (500182.BY) +0.6% at INR1,889. RIL (500325.BY) +0.6% at INR1,078. But, Larsen (500510.BY) down 0.7% at INR2,064 on profit-booking. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0407 GMT [Dow Jones] Daiwa Securities Capital Markets says robust earnings released by U.S. tech companies and Japan's August machinery orders (which beat consensus expectations) should provide some support for Tokyo stocks (short-term). Nonetheless, notes the most actively traded names which were expected to lead the market up on Wednesday were mostly losers, suggesting clouded visibility for stock market's direction. In light of historical precedent, thinks Japan could benefit from sustained easy credit, in-line with monetary policy taken by U.S. Fed Chairman Bernanke (an authority on the Great Depression), and eventually overcome ongoing prolonged deflation. Adds the recent steep yen rise could cause Japan to step up efforts to counter deflationary trends, possibly providing good entry point into Tokyo stock market. USD/JPY hit fresh 15-year low today (81.17); Nikkei currently up 1.9% at 9580.13. (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0406 GMT [Dow Jones] Power rationing at aluminum producers since August in Henan, Guangxi, Guizhou, Qinghai provinces has so far affected around 800,000 tons of aluminum production, helping offset high inventories in spot market, keep prices well-supported, says Beijing-based trader; "a supply shortage actually triggered destocking at end-users," he says, estimating more than 100,000 tons of aluminum have been consumed in existing stocks in past three months, one-tenth of estimated aluminum inventory on spot market. Notes spot aluminum has been trading steady against SHFE front-month contract, recently at premium of around CNY70/ton, indicating solid buying activities. Benchmark January contract closes midday down 0.3% at CNY16,550/ton, front-month October contract down 0.2% at CNY16,120/ton. (yue.li@dowjones.com) [ 10-14-10 0036ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101014e6ae0003r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
