\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/CNY Extends Fall; 6.6500 May Support\b0\par\par\uc2 1,646 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2010\par 06:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0424 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY extends fall to fresh intraday low under current system midday on broad USD weakness in Asia; on OTC, USD/CNY at 6.6546 vs 6.6641 last close; 6.6500 may support. "The record low central parity rate today was driven by the weak dollar index," says Shanghai-based trader at European bank. PBOC sets fixing at record low 6.6582 vs 6.6693 yesterday. "But trading range in the morning hovers around the parity rate level as people aren't sure how the customer flows may drive the market later in the session." Offshore, 1-year USD/CNY at 6.4380/6.4440 vs 6.4580/6.4630 late yesterday. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0418 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei +2.0% at 9593.83, staying firm even after USD/JPY sinks to fresh 15-year low (now 81.25). "The market is being supported by expectations for more liquidity on the back of anticipated large-scale U.S. monetary easing and following Japan's drastic easing steps," says \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2  strategist Hideyuki Ishiguro. Adds foreign investors' risk tolerance is increasing. Pegs near-term Nikkei resistance at 9700 on assumption \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Support seen at 9400 if USD/JPY doesn't fall below Y80. All 33 Topix subindexes higher. Real estate sector best performer (+4.1%) following robust Tokyo area September new condo sales data. \uc2 Mitsui Fudosan\uc2  (8801.TO) up 4.3% to Y1,620 and Mitsubishi Estate (8802.TO) up 4.8% to Y1,537. Dentsu (4324.TO) up 5.6% at Y1,970 on \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  rating hike. \uc2 Shinsei Bank\uc2  (8303.TO) off 5.3% at Y71 on profit-taking following recent sharp gains. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0417 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  maintains cautiously optimistic outlook for Hong Kong economy after Wednesday Policy Address. Says yet to be seen whether latest policy initiatives (includes boosting land supply) will help alleviate challenges in housing. But "Hong Kong has had to cope with considerable volatility in monetary conditions and asset markets, which has resulted in undesirably large fluctuations in economic activity, or boom-bust economic cycles," says house. "The recent gains in asset (equity as well as property) markets amidst renewed expectations of further quantitative easing from developed markets has further heightened our concerns for excess volatility and instability ahead when conditions reverse." Adds, expansion of offshore CNY financial services in Hong Kong represents significant opportunities for economy in medium term, increasing circulation of CNY could complicate monetary management in Hong Kong, with considerable implications on asset markets. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0409 GMT [Dow Jones]India government bonds tad higher as Bank of Korea's decision to keep its policy rate steady raises hopes RBI will think twice before conducting any future rate hikes as concerns over pace of global economic recovery remains, says trader with state-run bank; 7.80% 2020 bond at 98.65 vs 98.58 last close. But inflation remains key driver of rate expectations and cue from weekly price print later in day could hurt market sentiment ahead of monthly WPI due Friday. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0408 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CAD touches parity on continued broad-based USD weakness amid expectations for further quantitative easing by Fed, says Nobuaki Kubo, senior dealer at \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  Investment Services. Adding to those expecta