\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Nikkei +2.0%; Resistance Pegged At 9700\b0\par\par\uc2 1,701 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2010\par 06:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0418 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei +2.0% at 9593.83, staying firm even after USD/JPY sinks to fresh 15-year low (now 81.25). "The market is being supported by expectations for more liquidity on the back of anticipated large-scale U.S. monetary easing and following Japan's drastic easing steps," says \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2  strategist Hideyuki Ishiguro. Adds foreign investors' risk tolerance is increasing. Pegs near-term Nikkei resistance at 9700 on assumption \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Support seen at 9400 if USD/JPY doesn't fall below Y80. All 33 Topix subindexes higher. Real estate sector best performer (+4.1%) following robust Tokyo area September new condo sales data. \uc2 Mitsui Fudosan\uc2  (8801.TO) up 4.3% to Y1,620 and Mitsubishi Estate (8802.TO) up 4.8% to Y1,537. Dentsu (4324.TO) up 5.6% at Y1,970 on \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  rating hike. \uc2 Shinsei Bank\uc2  (8303.TO) off 5.3% at Y71 on profit-taking following recent sharp gains. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0417 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  maintains cautiously optimistic outlook for Hong Kong economy after Wednesday Policy Address. Says yet to be seen whether latest policy initiatives (includes boosting land supply) will help alleviate challenges in housing. But "Hong Kong has had to cope with considerable volatility in monetary conditions and asset markets, which has resulted in undesirably large fluctuations in economic activity, or boom-bust economic cycles," says house. "The recent gains in asset (equity as well as property) markets amidst renewed expectations of further quantitative easing from developed markets has further heightened our concerns for excess volatility and instability ahead when conditions reverse." Adds, expansion of offshore CNY financial services in Hong Kong represents significant opportunities for economy in medium term, increasing circulation of CNY could complicate monetary management in Hong Kong, with considerable implications on asset markets. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0409 GMT [Dow Jones]India government bonds tad higher as Bank of Korea's decision to keep its policy rate steady raises hopes RBI will think twice before conducting any future rate hikes as concerns over pace of global economic recovery remains, says trader with state-run bank; 7.80% 2020 bond at 98.65 vs 98.58 last close. But inflation remains key driver of rate expectations and cue from weekly price print later in day could hurt market sentiment ahead of monthly WPI due Friday. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0408 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CAD touches parity on continued broad-based USD weakness amid expectations for further quantitative easing by Fed, says Nobuaki Kubo, senior dealer at \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  Investment Services. Adding to those expectations, USD also weighed by \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's surprise decision to tighten its monetary policy, which contrasts sharply with mounting expectations for U.S. Federal Reserve to soon conduct more monetary easing. Adds, broad gains in Asian share markets also prompting investors to increase bets in commodity-linked \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as CAD, as gold hits new record high. USD/CAD last 1.0005. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0407 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex up 0.4% at 20,764.19, just off 20,854.55 early high - highest level since Jan. 15, 2008, buoyed by sustained buying from institutions, positive regional markets. Gains led by software, select metal plays. \uc2 Infosys (500209.BY) \uc2 +1.9% at INR3,208.10 after +2.5% yesterday - company to announce 2Q results tomorrow. "Liquidity is chasing share prices. Software and automobile shares are seeing a fair amount of buying on expectations of strong second quarter numbers," says dealer at domestic brokerage. Adds, volume picking up. Tips Sensex in 20,660-20,880 range. Says, some profit-taking could come in later as markets now +3.0% from Tuesday's close. Wipro (507685.BY) +1.5% at INR490, \uc2 Tata Steel\uc2  (500470.BY) +2.0% at INR651. Jindal Steel (532286.BY) +1.2% at INR727.35, \uc2 Hero Honda\uc2  (500182.BY) +0.6% at INR1,889. RIL (500325.BY) +0.6% at INR1,078. But, Larsen (500510.BY) down 0.7% at INR2,064 on profit-booking. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0407 GMT [Dow Jones] Daiwa Securities Capital Markets says robust earnings released by U.S. tech companies and Japan's August machinery orders (which beat consensus expectations) should provide some support for Tokyo stocks (short-term). Nonetheless, notes the most actively traded names which were expected to lead the market up on Wednesday were mostly losers, suggesting clouded visibility for stock market's direction. In light of historical precedent, thinks Japan could benefit from sustained easy credit, in-line with monetary policy taken by U.S. Fed Chairman Bernanke (an authority on the Great Depression), and eventually overcome ongoing prolonged deflation. Adds the recent steep yen rise could cause Japan to step up efforts to counter deflationary trends, possibly providing good entry point into Tokyo stock market. USD/JPY hit fresh 15-year low today (81.17); Nikkei currently up 1.9% at 9580.13. (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0406 GMT [Dow Jones] Power rationing at aluminum producers since August in Henan, Guangxi, Guizhou, Qinghai provinces has so far affected around 800,000 tons of aluminum production, helping offset high inventories in spot market, keep prices well-supported, says Beijing-based trader; "a supply shortage actually triggered destocking at end-users," he says, estimating more than 100,000 tons of aluminum have been consumed in existing stocks in past three months, one-tenth of estimated aluminum inventory on spot market. Notes spot aluminum has been trading steady against SHFE front-month contract, recently at premium of around CNY70/ton, indicating solid buying activities. Benchmark January contract closes midday down 0.3% at CNY16,550/ton, front-month October contract down 0.2% at CNY16,120/ton. (yue.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0404 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan MOF sells Y552.8 billion of 2.0% 30-year bonds with lowest price of 100.35, higher than market expectations of 100.20, yielding 1.981%; bid-to-cover ratio improves to 5.40--highest level since 30-year JGBs started to be auctioned in price-competitive style--vs 4.15 at previous auction in September. Tail also shorter at 0.10 vs 0.41 at last tender. Strong auction results suggest that life insurers, main buyers of superlong zone, may have lowered target yields amid ultra-easy financial conditions and banks, which usually active in medium-term zone, may have become more interested in 30-year sector as well, says Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities strategist Katsutoshi Inadome. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0359 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares up midday, but narrow early gains; banks still leading rise despite a report in state-run China Securities Journal said Chinese officials' inspection of loans to local-government financing platforms found CNY2 trillion worth at high risk of default. Report viewed as unsurprising; also report says the loans identified as risky amount to 4% of China's total outstanding loans, government has funds to support lenders. Shanghai Composite Index +0.4% at 2873.84, off intraday high at 2919.41; tipped to trade around 2850-2900 level for remainder of session. "Money that has been restricted from investing in to property (due to recent tightening measures) is going into the stock market, so the ample liquidity will support the index for awhile," says Amy Lin from Capital Securities. \uc2 China Merchants Bank\uc2  (600036.SH) +2.1% at CNY14.80, Bank of China (601988.SH) +1.5% at CNY3.50. Shenzhen Composite Index down 1.4% at 1212.42. (esther.fung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0358 GMT [Dow Jones] Chief executive of Australia's biggest bank Commonwealth Bank (CBA.AU), Ralph Norris, joins counterparts at \uc2 Westpac\uc2  (WBC.AU), \uc2 Australia & New Zealand Banking Group\uc2  (ANZ.AU) in foreshadowing interest rate rises by the banks beyond any moves in Australia's official cash rate. "I think there's no doubt that when we look at current funding costs, rates are going to increase," Norris tells reporters after a business lunch. "The additional cost of liquidity and the additional cost of capital is going to have an upward pressure on interest rates going forward." Norris declines to comment on when the bank may raise its rates and by how much. (Ross.Kelly@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0356 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR slips as regional cues weigh on pair, while stocks continue to climb boosting talk of more stock-directed USD supply, says trader with private bank; pair at 44.24 vs 44.52 late Wednesday. "There's clearly supply in the market, so now what the market is watching for is whether the RBI comes in at some point," says trader. But if RBI stays out, downside clearly remains, with eventual test of 43.80 over next few sessions appearing almost inevitable if global risk-on trend continues. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0337 GMT [Dow Jones] LME metals up across the board but gains purely USD driven with fundamentals not much of a factor, says Hong Kong-based broker. "Everything's up but there's not much happening apart from a bit of buying in copper," he says. LME 3-month copper at $8,453/oz, up $91 at 27-month high with 1,449 lots done after EUR/USD broke 1.40 around 0000 GMT; now at 1.4078. Lead at highest level since January, Zinc at highest level since May but very little volume, speculative bids ahead of London rather than any concerted buying. "Any change of course in the dollar before the year-end could cause a pretty serious correction but I'm still bullish on copper into 2011. Maybe we will see $9000 or $10,000," he says. Copper mine supply expected to be stagnant next year. Chinese demand growth, any strengthening in OECD economies expected to spur record prices. Copper hit record $8,940/ton in early July 2008. (james.campbell@dowjones.com) [ 10-14-10 0018ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101014e6ae0003l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
