\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Thai Govt Bonds Higher; Local Players Buying\b0\par\par\uc2 1,589 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2010\par 05:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0316 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bonds extend gains on buying mainly by local players, while foreigners' buying subdued after Thai authorities' move to impose 15% tax on foreign investment in local bonds effective from yesterday. "Local investors anticipate the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will hold policy rate (at 1.75%) in October's meeting as a way to slow down the baht's rise," says bond dealer. BOT's policy rate meeting due Wednesday. Yields expected to fall 2-3 bps further rest of day. Bid/offer yields of issue due December 2015 quoted at 2.50%/2.45% vs 2.52%/2.49% late yesterday, November 2016 at 2.63%/2.58% vs 2.67%/2.62%, June 2019 at 2.855%/2.845% vs 2.88%/2.87%. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0311 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot silver at $24.29/oz, just off 30-year high at $24.37; has rallied 37% since August 24 when it broke out of previous range; gold/silver ratio has dropped from 69 to today's 56.5 in that time and could be due for some kind of reversal, says Mark van der Sluys, managing director at Bullionmark in Sydney. "Silver is being driven by new money coming into the market. There is investment money but there's also speculative cash chasing this." Adds could be worth buying gold/silver ratio as any correction/consolidation phase in precious metals almost certain to see gold outperform. Adds can't rule out more gains for gold, silver with shallowness of any dips testament to amount of new money seeking exposure to precious metals. "Even experienced traders are no longer waiting for a correction, they are seeing any sign of stability as a chance to accumulate a larger position." (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0308 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 +0.8% as risk appetite remains strong, with strong gains in regional markets supporting sentiment, says \uc2 Craigs Investment Partners\uc2  broker Belinda Stanley; "Offshore is anticipating a good earnings week coming from stocks in U.S. It started off relatively well so people are feeling rather buoyant even with the lukewarm economic data coming out both offshore and here." Says good sentiment helping heavyweights; Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ) +1.4% at NZ$8.30, Telecom NZ (TEL.NZ) +2.5% at NZ$2.07, \uc2 Contact Energy\uc2  (CEN.NZ) +0.4% at NZ$5.68. NZX-50 last 3257.30. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0306 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR likely to open lower as MAS tightening in Singapore drags on USD/Asians, while EUR rally, upbeat global stock moves also likely to intensify pressure, says trader with foreign bank. Tips pair to open in 44.20-44.25 band, vs 44.52 late yesterday, and trade in 44.10-44.40 band in session. "The key is how strong a support oil importer-led buying provides, because otherwise the cues point to a pretty strong INR rally," says trader. But adds RBI support unlikely at this stage, as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may avoid bucking global USD correction for now. Foreign funds net buyers of a provisional INR21.03 billion worth of stocks yesterday. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0246 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW sustains fall, last at 1,112.7 vs 1,120.7 late yesterday in Seoul despite BOK decision to keep key policy rate on hold at 2.25% earlier in day, as monetary tightening in Singapore sends USD tumbling vs regionals, weighing on pair, traders say, with many in market anticipating a test of key 1,110 support later in day. "The Australian dollar as well as the euro is surging, while foreigners are chalking up local stocks worth more than KRW200 billion in net so far to day. The pair's got to fall more," say local bank trader. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0242 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY hits fresh 15-year low at 81.28 as broad-based USD weakness, exacerbated earlier by Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  monetary tightening, continues to weigh on pair. But investors' continued caution over possible JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities may keep pair supported above 81.00 for rest of global day, says Satoshi Okagawa, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at \uc2 Sumitomo Mitsui Banking Corporation\uc2 . Says strong Asian share markets may also offer support as JPY loses safe-haven sheen. For these reasons, says "it wouldn't be strange if we start seeing more bids on the dollar-yen now." Pair last at 81.32. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0240 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares extend gains, led by banks after a bad loans investigation by regulators indicated nothing new, analysts say. Shanghai Composite Index +1.7% at 2910.62, faces immediate resistance at 2950. "Banks are leading because they already pre-digested risks from bad loans to local governments. Today after the report came out, the loan risks are more certain and there appears to be no other risks to worry about," says Zhang Zhuo at Minsheng Securities. \uc2 China Merchants Bank\uc2  (600036.SH) +3.7% at CNY15.02, Bank of China (601988.SH) +2.3% at CNY3.53. Shenzhen Composite Index flat at 1229.48. (esther.fung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0238 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore MAS's implied Singapore dollar slope now likely changed to 3% annual appreciation vs 2% earlier, ING economist Tim Condon says in note after MAS unexpectedly tightens by raising slope "slightly", widens undisclosed band "in view of the volatility across international financial markets". Condon notes "we think the (widening) move is designed to deflect potential criticism that heavy exchange market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - the US$27 billion YTD pace is a record - was depressing the Singapore dollar." Tips 3-month target for USD/SGD at 1.2850 vs 1.2920 earlier forecast. Pair last 1.2917, quoted at record low 1.2886 soon after MAS statement vs 1.3050 late Asia yesterday. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0233 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei 225 December futures up 1.8% at 9580 as foreign investors buy despite weaker USD/JPY (now 81.34), following Tuesday's sharp fall in Nikkei (cash market lost 2.1%, futures down 1.9%), market analysts say. "The drop (on Tuesday) was too heavy. Also, Japan stocks have underperformed overseas markets, so people are buying back," says Japanese brokerage manager. Says index may hover around Friday's close of 9580 (last trading day before Tuesday's fall), market analysts say. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0231 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI +1.1% at 21,717.42, earlier touching 23,788.94, highest intraday level since June 6, 2008, tracking regional gain on Wall Street cue. Volume hefty at HK$21.25 billion so far, full-day volume set to crack HK$100 billion mark again. HKEx (0388.HK) clear beneficiary of busy market activities, +2.9% at HK$174.90. Taifook Securities says despite good run recently, HSI, H-share index at Wednesday's close risen only 7.0%, 4.0%, respectively, year-to-date; "remain at relatively undemanding valuations" of 15.0X, 13.8X forward earnings. HK developers shrug off HK government's latest sector-curbing measures announced in policy address as viewed as not that effective; \uc2 Sino Land\uc2  (0083.HK) +3.2% at HK$16.90, \uc2 SHK Properties\uc2  (0016.HK) +1.0% at HK$137.30. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0229 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 1.8% at 9574.78 midday on futures buying and sharp gains in commodity-linked shares. In rare development, exporters up despite further yen appreciation (USD/JPY now 81.35), though some analysts say index merely recouping Tuesday's 2.1% loss. "There seems to be a temperature difference between the impact of a stronger yen on the Japanese economy as a whole and on individual exporters," says Takashi Ushio, general manager at \uc2 Marusan Securities\uc2 , noting some signs of improvement in individual company efforts to tackle \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk. Adds Nikkei unlikely to rise further above 9600 amid lack of fresh buying incentives. All 33 Topix subindexes higher; \uc2 JX Holdings\uc2  (5020.TO) up 5.6% at Y488 and \uc2 Sumitomo Metal Mining\uc2  (5713.TO) up 3.4% at Y1,444 on gains in crude and gold prices. Among exporters, \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  (7203.TO) up 2.4% at Y2,913 and Kyocera (6971.TO) up 3.0% at Y8,630. \uc2 Yahoo Japan\uc2  (4689.TO) up 5.5% at Y30,350 following WSJ report that AOL (AOL) and several private equity groups are exploring possibility of buying \uc2 Yahoo! (\uc2 YHOO). (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0228 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares advancing, with investor interest stoked by gains in overseas markets; benchmark PSEi +1.1% to 4239.06 in moderate volume, with gainers beating decliners 81 to 27. Resistance pegged at 4250. Gainers led by Metropolitan Bank & Trust (MBT.PH), up 0.2% at PHP73.95, after announcing PHP10 billion rights offer, conglomerate Alliance Global Group (AGI.PH), up 1.8% at PHP11.22, after its property unit Megaworld (MEG.PH), up 2.6% at PHP2.39, announced 81% on-year surge in real estate sales, \uc2 Aboitiz Power\uc2  (AP.PH), up 1.8% at PHP25.25. "Overseas markets are up and that adds to the optimism over earnings prospects," says AB Capital research head Jose Vistan. (cris.larano@dowjones.com) [ 10-13-10 2316ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101014e6ae0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
