yesterday. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0233 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei 225 December futures up 1.8% at 9580 as foreign investors buy despite weaker USD/JPY (now 81.34), following Tuesday's sharp fall in Nikkei (cash market lost 2.1%, futures down 1.9%), market analysts say. "The drop (on Tuesday) was too heavy. Also, Japan stocks have underperformed overseas markets, so people are buying back," says Japanese brokerage manager. Says index may hover around Friday's close of 9580 (last trading day before Tuesday's fall), market analysts say. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0231 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI +1.1% at 21,717.42, earlier touching 23,788.94, highest intraday level since June 6, 2008, tracking regional gain on Wall Street cue. Volume hefty at HK$21.25 billion so far, full-day volume set to crack HK$100 billion mark again. HKEx (0388.HK) clear beneficiary of busy market activities, +2.9% at HK$174.90. Taifook Securities says despite good run recently, HSI, H-share index at Wednesday's close risen only 7.0%, 4.0%, respectively, year-to-date; "remain at relatively undemanding valuations" of 15.0X, 13.8X forward earnings. HK developers shrug off HK government's latest sector-curbing measures announced in policy address as viewed as not that effective; \uc2 Sino Land\uc2  (0083.HK) +3.2% at HK$16.90, \uc2 SHK Properties\uc2  (0016.HK) +1.0% at HK$137.30. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0229 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 1.8% at 9574.78 midday on futures buying and sharp gains in commodity-linked shares. In rare development, exporters up despite further yen appreciation (USD/JPY now 81.35), though some analysts say index merely recouping Tuesday's 2.1% loss. "There seems to be a temperature difference between the impact of a stronger yen on the Japanese economy as a whole and on individual exporters," says Takashi Ushio, general manager at \uc2 Marusan Securities\uc2 , noting some signs of improvement in individual company efforts to tackle \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk. Adds Nikkei unlikely to rise further above 9600 amid lack of fresh buying incentives. All 33 Topix subindexes higher; \uc2 JX Holdings\uc2  (5020.TO) up 5.6% at Y488 and \uc2 Sumitomo Metal Mining\uc2  (5713.TO) up 3.4% at Y1,444 on gains in crude and gold prices. Among exporters, \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  (7203.TO) up 2.4% at Y2,913 and Kyocera (6971.TO) up 3.0% at Y8,630. \uc2 Yahoo Japan\uc2  (4689.TO) up 5.5% at Y30,350 following WSJ report that AOL (AOL) and several private equity groups are exploring possibility of buying \uc2 Yahoo! (\uc2 YHOO). (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0228 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares advancing, with investor interest stoked by gains in overseas markets; benchmark PSEi +1.1% to 4239.06 in moderate volume, with gainers beating decliners 81 to 27. Resistance pegged at 4250. Gainers led by Metropolitan Bank & Trust (MBT.PH), up 0.2% at PHP73.95, after announcing PHP10 billion rights offer, conglomerate Alliance Global Group (AGI.PH), up 1.8% at PHP11.22, after its property unit Megaworld (MEG.PH), up 2.6% at PHP2.39, announced 81% on-year surge in real estate sales, \uc2 Aboitiz Power\uc2  (AP.PH), up 1.8% at PHP25.25. "Overseas markets are up and that adds to the optimism over earnings prospects," says AB Capital research head Jose Vistan. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0228 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi +0.9% at 1892.74 following \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to keep benchmark interest rate unchanged at 2.25% amid uncertainty over global economic recovery, rising concerns over KRW strength; "The Bank of Korea may have decided to freeze the key rate before the G20 summit next month in Seoul as advanced countries are moving toward quantitative easing," says Bae Sung-young at \uc2 Hyundai Securities\uc2 ; tips resistance at 1900. Foreign investors net buyers today, picking up KRW219 billion worth of shares so far, expected to buy KRW350 billion-KRW400 billion. Intel's (INTC) better-than-expected 3Q earnings supporting domestic tech plays; \uc2 Samsung SDI\uc2  (006400.SE) +1.7% at KRW151,500, \uc2 Samsung Electro-Mechanics\uc2  (009150.SE) +3.5% at KRW120,000, LG Display (034220.SE) +2.6% at KRW39,600. Auto stocks also rising as stronger JPY expected boost price competitiveness (vs Japanese automakers); \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) +2.2% at KRW165,500, \uc2 Kia Motors\uc2  (000270.SE) +1.7% at KRW39,500, \uc2 Ssangyong Motor\uc2  (003620.SE) +1.2% at KRW9,230. (kyong-ae.choi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0223 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares expected to climb further, tracking gains in most external bourses amid expectations of U.S. quantitative easing. "The SET Index will likely test the 1000 psychological level today, but whether it can stay firmly above the level remains in question as retail investors may look to take profit around that level," says BFIT Securities strategist Aphisit Limsupanark. Gains likely led by heavyweight blue chips in energy, banking sectors. SET expected in 986-1002 range after ending +1.6% at 992.60 yesterday. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0220 GMT [Dow Jones] Fueled by weaker USD, spot gold hits fresh record high at $1,378.60/oz, before easing to $1,376.10, up $4.20 from late NY. Meteoric rise in gold prices definitely impressive, says Phillip Futures; "and further gains today are likely on follow-through strength as some late participants enter the market in a flurry." Reckons skeptical investors earlier concerned gold rally may be losing steam may also be convinced to enter bull camp. Says next price target is $1,400. Gold continues to take its cue from USD, and further weakening may be positive for gold, says house; pegs support at $1,365. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com) [ 10-13-10 2306ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101014e6ae0002m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoa