\par\par \b \uc2 MONEY TALKS: Global  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Spat Has No Winners\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shri Navaratnam  \par \uc2 A Dow Jones Newswires Column  \par \uc2 630 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Oktober 2010\par 09:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Global policymakers have little to gain by escalating the tensions in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Indeed, any "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war" is likely to be a phony one.\par \par \uc2 International Monetary Fund Managing Director Dominique Strauss-Kahn is right: "It's fair to say that many do consider their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a weapon, and that's certainly not for the good of the global economy," he said last week.\par \par \uc2 The warning came amid an escalation in rhetoric by politicians and other policymakers, which could harden attitudes as countries try to give their exchange rates a relative advantage.\par \par \uc2 China has been singled out for criticism by the U.S. and others for an undervalued yuan, though the finger-pointing can be a dangerous game when many others are also working in some fashion to keep a lid on their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Most emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been rising against the dollar, and that's not China's fault. However, with the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  falling broadly and China continuing to hold the yuan artificially low, tensions have risen over the misalignment in global \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Federal Reserve's apparent willingness to soon embark on a second round of quantitative easing to breathe life into the world's largest economy will essentially debase the dollar. The Fed's September minutes Tuesday showed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is leaning toward more unconventional easing at its policy meeting next month.\par \par \uc2 The weakening dollar has forced many countries, including Korea, Singapore, Taiwan and Thailand, to curb their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect their export-reliant economies. In the most high-profile example, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the first time in six years last month to cap the yen, as it surged to a 15-year high against the greenback.\par \par \uc2 Confronted by an uneven recovery in the global economy, many countries are eager to protect their exports. The concern is that policymakers will intentionally try to weaken their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , further raising the friction.\par \par \uc2 It will be far more productive for nations to work together to support the global economy as it emerges from the worst recession since the Great Depression. Indeed, with many countries tapped into global trade and relying on each other to feed their economies, policymakers are likely to see reason sooner rather than later.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner says there's "no risk" of a global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war. Geithner, on the Charlie Rose talk show, continued to call on China to let the yuan rise, saying the U.S. wants to make sure the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciates "at a gradual but still significant rate."\par \par \uc2 Next month's summit of the Group of 20 developed and developing nations in Seoul is shaping up to be a tense affair as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  spat takes center stage.\par \par \uc2 This need not happen. The financial crisis showed that nations can work together for the common goal of economic stability.\par \par \uc2 Softening the belligerent tone might be a good starting point for policymakers.\par \par \uc2 (Shri Navaratnam is Asia-Pacific Managing Editor for Markets news at Dow Jones Newswires. He has 15 years of experience as a journalist and editor, having worked for news organizations in New Zealand, Singapore and Sri Lanka. He can be contacted on 65-64154-147 or by email: shri.navaratnam@dowjones.com.)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 13-10-10 0757GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1171 5008 70779  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101013e6ad000m0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
