\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Germany Warns China Stance On Yuan Risks Trade War\b0\par\par\uc2 770 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Oktober 2010\par 12:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Adds background, context.)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Andrea Thomas \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 BERLIN (Dow Jones)--Europe's largest exporter kept up pressure on China to more fairly align its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with those of its trading partners, warning that a trade war could result from countries competing to aid exports by holding down their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 German Economy Minister Rainer Bruederle said it is up to China to prevent a damaging trade dispute with the U.S.\par \par \uc2 "We have to take care that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war doesn't become a trade war," Bruederle told the Wednesday edition of Handelsblatt daily. The ministry later confirmed the comments. "China bears a lot of responsibility for ensuring that it doesn't come to an escalation," he said.\par \par \uc2 Bruederle, who is currently in China, will represent Germany at next week's meeting of ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors from the Group of 20 industrial and developing nations in South Korea. That meeting will be the next venue for policy makers to address perceived \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  imbalances that distort trade.\par \par \uc2 Last weekend's meeting of the International Monetary Fund in Washington became a forum for complaints about so-called competitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation, without a consensus on how to act against it. China, for one, has declined to alter monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 Beijing has been singled out. But recent monetary steps by Japan, Brazil, Thailand, Australia and others also could have the effect of bringing down their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values.\par \par \uc2 Germany and other European Union countries have followed in the footsteps of the U.S. in protesting that China is cultivating an unfair trade advantage by refusing to allow its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values to drift higher, which would make Chinese goods more expensive.\par \par \uc2 Europe's economic recovery has depended largely on export trade to big emerging markets such as China. As the euro soars to new highs against the dollar and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of other trading partners, making European goods more expensive, that source of growth could dry out.\par \par \uc2 Bruederle declined to be drawn on what counter-measures could be taken to offset China's policy of keeping the yuan's exchange rate low, which some western governments--above all the U.S.--say give the country's exports an unfair competitive advantage.\par \par \uc2 He warned that punitive import duties, which U.S. lawmakers are currently considering, would "only lead to retaliation. We can all only lose through protectionist measures."\par \par \uc2 Bruederle's unease comes after Germany's top central banker said Tuesday that China manipulates its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Some critics say the yuan is 40% undervalued.\par \par \uc2 "China has a current-account surplus because its authorities manipulate their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Axel Weber, Bundesbank president and ECB Governing Council member, in a question-and-answer session after a speech in New York.\par \par \uc2 In fact, for years, Europe has called for a more flexible Chinese exchange-rate regime, because the strength of the euro, which has recently hit an eight-month high against the dollar, is making goods less competitive on global markets. Germany would be particularly vulnerable to any possible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and trade war because of its strong export sector.\par \par \uc2 Hans Heinrich Driftmann, president of the DIHK German Chambers of Commerce, warned that attempts to gain a competitive edge by keeping a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a low value could result in new trade barriers.\par \par \uc2 "At the end, there would be only losers," Driftmann warned in Tuesday's online edition of the monthly manager magazin.\par \par \uc2 Chancellor Angela Merkel wants China to let its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reflect its true value, her spokesman said Friday, but he also included the U.S. in his criticism and said that Washington might keep the dollar undervalued.\par \par \uc2 China should make its exchange-rate regime more flexible so that the global financial system isn't subject to volatility and instability, European Commissioner for Economic and Monetary Affairs Olli Rehn said Saturday.\par \par \uc2 China in mid-June ended a two-year peg of the yuan to the dollar, but has since let its tightly managed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise just 2%. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  publicly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Sept. 15, selling some Y2 trillion.\par \par \uc2 "Going forward, risks of excess volatility and disorderly movements in exchange rates should be avoided in view of their adverse implications for economic and financial stability," Rehn said during a speech before the IMF's policy-steering committee. "In this respect, the Chinese authorities are encouraged to implement soon a more flexible exchange-rate regime for the [yuan]."\par \par \uc2 -By Andrea Thomas, Dow Jones Newswires; +49-30-2888-4126; andrea.thomas@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Geoffrey Smith in Frankfurt and Riva Froymovich in New York contributed to this article.) [ 13-10-10 1047GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101013e6ad000wq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
