\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECT: WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Falls But Euro Struggles At $1.40\b0\par\par\uc2 677 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Oktober 2010\par 12:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 ("CORRECT: WORLD \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Falls But Euro Struggles At $1.40," at 0942 GMT, misstated the dollar/yen levels in the 11th paragraph. The correct version follows:)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  \par  \par    By Nicholas Hastings \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--The dollar tumbled again Wednesday as the minutes of the last Federal Open Market Committee meeting raised speculation that more quantitative easing is on the way.\par \par \uc2 The euro, meanwhile, jumped back up to $1.40 after Axel Weber, the Bundesbank president and front-runner to be the next European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president, said that the bank's bond purchase program "should now be phased out permanently."\par \par \uc2 This helped to confirm that while the U.S. is on the verge of more monetary easing, the euro zone is preparing to exit its unconventional monetary strategy.\par \par \uc2 The dollar's decline on the FOMC minutes could accelerate later this week if the rash of new U.S. data scheduled for release increases doubts about the U.S. economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 The Fed is widely expected to introduce more quantitative easing, starting at its next meeting Nov. 3, depending on the strength or weakness of the economy.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts, however, noted that the large number of short positions in the dollar means that the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could still experience some short-covering in the near term.\par \par \uc2 Despite its initial momentum, the euro is still having difficulty making a sustained break over $1.40, a resistance level that it has tackled and failed to break twice before.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    See how the euro failed to climb over $1.40: \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4736"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4736}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 Apart from the dollar's weakness, the euro is also benefiting from a general improvement in market sentiment, with small rallies in Asian stock markets followed by gains in most European bourses.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 News from Japan that machinery orders rose by 10.1% in August, instead of falling by 4.5% as expected, contributed to the brighter mood and the increased appetite for risk.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After rising to $1.4001, the euro slipped back to trade at $1.3990 at midmorning, still well up from $1.3914 late Tuesday in New York, according to EBS.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was also up at Y114.59 from Y113.74, while the dollar rose a little to Y81.90 from Y81.74.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Market participants remain wary of further \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stop the yen's appreciation even though Prime Minister Naoto Kan admitted that maneuvering \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is not consistent with G-20 policy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar's general weakness drove it down slightly against the Swiss franc to CHF0.9562 from CHF0.9570, with the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at one stage hitting a new all-time low of CHF0.9544.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The pound rallied to $1.5883 from $1.5795. Apart from dollar weakness, sterling got some support from an announcement by Standard Chartered that it is leading the way in boosting its capital to meet new banking requirements with a GBP3.3 billion rights issue.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The issue could well pave the way for more recapitalization funding by other major European banks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the other hand, the pound didn't get any help from unemployment data showing a small monthly rise in the claimant count in September. This suggests the labor market is deteriorating again even before the public-sector job cuts that are expected to kick in early next year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Also, earlier Wednesday, Nationwide reported that its latest consumer confidence index was at its lowest in over a year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Nicholas Hastings, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 20 7842 9493; nick.hastings@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkBackEurope@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 13-10-10 1013GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1035 28038 4163 44462   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101013e6ad000uk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
