\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:Nymex Crude Biased Up Near Term;$83.50 Initial Cap\b0\par\par\uc2 1,637 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2010\par 01:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2342 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude likely to consolidate with risks skewed to upside after settling up $1.34 yesterday at $83.01/bbl, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. Daily continuation chart positive-biased as MACD bullish, stochastics revert to bullish mode at overbought level; 5-day & 15-day moving averages rising. Resistance at $83.50 (Monday's high); breach would expose upside to $84.43 (5-month high set Oct. 7), then psychological round-numbered levels of $85.00, $86.00, $87.00, then $87.15 (May 3 peak). Support at $82.33 (previous cap set Tuesday); breach would expose downside to $80.88 (Tuesday's low), then $80.30 (Friday's low) - below which would open way down to $79.70 (Oct. 1 low), then $78.04 (previous cap set Sept. 13, near 200-day moving average) and $77.38 (55-day moving average). November crude last up 35 cents at $83.36/bbl on Globex. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2336 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi may consolidate despite positive U.S. lead overnight ahead of BOK policy rate decision later in day. Mirae Asset Securities analyst Lee Jin-woo tips 1860-1886 range for now, says market may be volatile depending on what BOK decides, also notes futures options expire today; "I don't think the market will show a clear direction; positive factors like Intel's 3Q results (that boosted U.S. markets overnight) have already been factored in here. The majority opinion seems to be that the BOK will hold, but how the foreign investors react to a decision from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains to be seen." Banks, insurance plays like \uc2 Hana Financial\uc2  (086790.SE), Samsung Life (032830.SE) may rise should BOK decide to hike rates, while a decision to hold steady would help securities firms and construction plays like \uc2 Daewoo Securities\uc2  (006800.SE), GS Engineering (006360.SE). Kospi ends 0.4% higher at 1876.15 yesterday. (vincent.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2334 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at 10 foreign brokerages place net buy orders for 5.5 million Japanese shares overnight, according to traders; 1st day of premarket buying after 1 day of net selling may be positive for market at open, although yen value basis of figures unknown. Buy orders total 21.6 million shares, with sell orders amounting to 16.1 million shares. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2333 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 +0.6% on strong lead from U.S. market, says Hamilton Hindin Greene broker James Smalley; "But that pattern of when the markets fall we don't fall as much and when the markets go up we don't go up as much seems to be continuing here today." Adds sentiment buoys Telecom NZ(TEL.NZ), +2.0% to NZ$2.06. AMP Office Trust (APT.NZ) down 1.3% at NZ$0.79 as stock trading ex-dividend. Rakon (RAK.NZ)+2.4% at NZ$1.26; says this follows Apple's share price rising above the $300 mark for 1st time--Rakon provides crystals for some Apple products. NZX-50 last 3251.15. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2333 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW may fall vs 1,120.7 late yesterday in Seoul as USD continued to weaken on global markets overnight on prevailing market view U.S. Fed will introduce more quantitative easing measures near term. Key focus today likely to be BOK's rate decision due around 0100 GMT. Market split over whether BOK will hike or stand pat; 9 of 17 economists, fixed-income analysts surveyed by Dow Jones expect BOK to hike key rate by 25 bps to 2.50%; rest tip an on-hold decision. Local bank trader tips pair in wide 1,100-1,125 range, expects KRW volatility depending on outcome of BOK monetary policy meeting, possible USD buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But notes, "the longer-term trend, which is for the pair to head lower, isn't likely to change much," regardless of meeting's outcome. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2332 GMT [Dow Jones] Market participants say USD level remains a key mover for base metals. "USD is the real driver currently. Keep an eye on the EUR/USD for some idea on base metal price direction again today," says major participant. U.S. corporate earnings season reports also expected to affect base metals, with U.S. equities a strong driver of base metals prices, it adds. Triland Metals says copper still being driven by hopes of Fed easing, resulting weaker USD. It also said that the high volatility traditionally follows LME week dinner didn't occur this year; says aluminum looks rather comfortably settled at these levels. "For sure if the week ends closing at this range the (technical) signal then will be strong for a continuation of the trend," it says. As for lead, ease of price breaking $2,400 suggests $2,500 near-term target. "Market doesn't feel like stopping this upward momentum," for now, it says. Yesterday, LME 3-month copper settled $22 higher on day at $8,362 ton, lead leapt $59 at $2,435, zinc rose $41 at $2,410, tin climbed $280 at $26,775, nickel added $400 to $24,400, but aluminum bucked trend losing $19 at $2,417. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2331 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. Treasury yields may fall due to ongoing expectations for more quantitative easing from Federal Reserve in early November to cope with slowing economic growth, so investors will pay close attention to U.S. data due later in day such as PPI, jobless claims, says senior trader at U.K. bank. Dow Jones surveys of economists tip September PPI +0.1% gain from August's +0.4%, jobless claims may rise by 1,000. Benchmark 10-year yield last at 2.424%, may trade in 2.400%-2.450%, he says.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2328 GMT [Dow Jones] USD may continue to weaken against EUR, JPY on lingering view that Fed may take additional quantitative easing measures, says senior dealer at major Japan bank. Says if U.S. economic indicators such as weekly jobless claims due 1230 GMT worse than expected, that may trigger further USD-selling. Says EUR/USD may rise to 1.4030 by end of global day, 8-month high marked last Thursday; if that level breached, expects stop-loss buying orders could be triggered, sending pair to 1.4100; last 1.3975. Tips resistance for USD/JPY at 81.00, last 81.80. Says EUR/JPY may trade in 113.80-114.50 range vs last 114.21.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2320 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead December JGB futures may dip ahead of 30-year bond auction later in day, but with prolonged JPY strength, JGBs should resume rising after tender if result shows firm demand as expected, says Mizuho Securities chief market analyst Tetsuya Miura. Still, upside also "a bit tough" as some investors keen to take profits on recent gains. Tips futures to trade in 143.65-144.00 range while 10-year yield may trade in 0.870%-0.895%. Futures last 143.91, 0.895%.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2306 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ September REINZ data, August retail sales figures paint picture of household sector in "balance sheet repair mode," says \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  economist Bernard Doyle; "Accordingly the message for the RBNZ is to take an early summer break and see how things look in 2011--no need for higher rates anytime soon." Adds retail sales data indicate "households keeping their hands in pockets" with department stores, hardware, appliances sectors all sequentially weaker. Adds, however, electronic card transaction data released earlier in week suggested bounce in September so may see improvement in September retail sales data. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2303 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei likely to open higher following overnight gains in U.S. stocks following strong earnings from \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase\uc2  (JPM) and Intel (INTC). But analysts say initial gains likely to see trimming as investors remain cautious about lingering firmer yen trend (USD/JPY at 81.80). Index range for today seen at 9300-9500 vs 9403.51 (+0.2%) yesterday. "International consensus regarding (\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market) is hard to obtain," says Yutaka Miura, senior technical analyst at Mizuho Securities. "Investors are still watching for an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  move, but the wariness is beginning to fade." Resource-linked shares likely to outperform on gains in gold and crude futures. Nikkei 225 December futures ended Chicago trading at 9500 vs Osaka close at 9410. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Views sharply divided over whether BOK will resume monetary tightening Thursday by raising policy rate after defying expectations for rate hike at 2 previous meetings; 9 of 17 economists, fixed-income analysts surveyed by Dow Jones expect BOK to hike key rate by 25 bps to 2.50%; balance 8 tip on-hold decision (with 4 of these predicting 25 bps hike before year-out, rest forecast no move until 1Q 2011). "Judging from economic fundamentals, the BOK should resume rate hikes after having paused for two months," says DBS Group in research note. But given latest capital inflows into Asia due to further quantitative easing in Japan, expectations Fed will follow soon, BOK likely to be "reluctant" to hike at this point as higher rates would invite more inflows and further pressure KRW higher, it adds. Rate decision due around 0100 GMT Thursday. (In-Soo.Nam@dowjones.com) [ 10-13-10 1942ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101013e6ad000ez\par }\page {\par\fs20