ter defying expectations for rate hike at 2 previous meetings; 9 of 17 economists, fixed-income analysts surveyed by Dow Jones expect BOK to hike key rate by 25 bps to 2.50%; balance 8 tip on-hold decision (with 4 of these predicting 25 bps hike before year-out, rest forecast no move until 1Q 2011). "Judging from economic fundamentals, the BOK should resume rate hikes after having paused for two months," says DBS Group in research note. But given latest capital inflows into Asia due to further quantitative easing in Japan, expectations Fed will follow soon, BOK likely to be "reluctant" to hike at this point as higher rates would invite more inflows and further pressure KRW higher, it adds. Rate decision due around 0100 GMT Thursday. (In-Soo.Nam@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2257 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold bid $1,373.40/oz, up $1.50 from late NY, holding nearly all of yesterday's gains, to be trading just below record high overnight of $1,374.50. In a technical analysis, \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  said although gains in price of gold in both EUR and USD terms impressive, and \uc2 Barclays\uc2  ultimately look for higher prices as this month and quarter progress, "near-term hurdles remain," with gold needing a sustained break of $1,368, year-to-date channel resistance. "Until then we can't rule out further near-term choppy activity," it says, pegging support at $1,324/$1,321 congestion zone of early October. But momentum, positioning, constructive seasonal pattern, "keep our 4Q focus higher, towards long-term channel resistance in the $1,440 area, into year-end," \uc2 Barclays\uc2  says. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2254 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional stock markets likely higher after DJIA posts 4th-straight gain to 5-month high; gold-, oil-related companies could shine as bullion, crude prices surge overnight. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, likely rise in equities, continued expectations for further quantitative easing from Fed likely to support EUR as well as commodities-related \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like AUD, NZD. EUR/USD at 1.3965 vs 1.3964 in late NY trade Wednesday, EUR/JPY at 114.22 vs 114.14, USD/JPY at 81.80 vs 81.74. On data slate: Japan CGPI at 2350 GMT, Australia consumer inflationary expectations survey at 0000 GMT, Singapore advance GDP estimates at 0000 GMT, MAS policy statement at 0000 GMT, Bank of Korea policy meeting and decision at 0130 GMT, Korea import, export price index at 0300 GMT. Later there's ECB bulletin, U.S. trade, PPI, weekly jobless claims, EIA oil report, foreign U.S. debt holdings, Minneapolis Fed president Kocherlakota speaks, German Ifo joint economic forecast. (colin.ng@dowjones.com) [ 10-13-10 1933ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101013e6ad000ev\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
