\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks Eke Out Gains, Boosted By Fed Minutes\b0\par\par\uc2 2,600 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Oktober 2010\par 00:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      11020.40  +10.06  +0.09% \par Nasdaq     2417.92  +15.59  +0.65% \par S&P 500    1169.77   +4.45  +0.38% \par FTSE 100   5661.59  -10.81  -0.19% \par Xetra DAX  6304.57   -4.94  -0.08% \par  \par 2Y Tsy  100       -1/32 0.367% +2.0BP \par 5Y Tsy  100 18/32 -4/32 1.130% +2.4BP \par 10Y Tsy 101 27/32 -4/32 2.411% +1.6BP \par  \par USD/JPY       81.76-80     -0.43% \par Range         82.34-81.66 \par EUR/USD       1.3916-22    +0.29% \par Range         1.3932-1.3774 \par AUD/USD       0.9861-66    +0.21% \par Range         0.9877-0.9768 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol    12.05-12.35% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol    13.15-13.50% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol    14.10-14.40% \par  \par Comex Gold          $1,346.70   -$7.70 \par Comex Gold Range    $1,341.40-$1,356.30 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $81.67   -$0.54 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks eked out a slim gain Tuesday after minutes from the September meeting of the Federal Reserve increased confidence that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will take further action to stimulate the economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average gained 10.06 points, or 0.09% to end at 11020.40. The Nasdaq Composite added 15.59, or 0.65%, to 2417.92 and the Standard & Poor's 500 index advanced 4.45, or 0.38%, to 1169.77.\par \par \uc2 After a morning in the red, stocks turned positive Tuesday after minutes from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's September meeting showed officials' discussions focused on buying more U.S. Treasurys or new strategies for inflation if prices remained too low and unemployment too high. The minutes also revealed that the Fed's staff cut projections for economic growth in 2011 and expected the underlying inflation rate--already below the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's informal objective--to slow further next year.\par \par \uc2 "What the Fed said today didn't surprise anybody," said Bill Vaughn, portfolio manager at Evercore Wealth Management. "Most people thought the comments were going to lay the groundwork for quantitative easing and they essentially did that."\par \par \uc2 Investors have been increasingly expecting the Fed to unveil additional stimulus measures at its November meeting, which has driven the dollar lower and stocks higher in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 Financial and technology companies led the gains in stocks as investors grew hopeful for earnings reports due from Intel and \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2 . Intel, which reported after Tuesday's close, gained 21 cents, or 1.1% to 19.77, while \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2 , which will release its report Wednesday morning, climbed 67 cents, or 1.7%, to 40.40. Meanwhile, \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  led the Dow, gaining 37 cents, or 2.8%, to 13.52.\par \par \uc2 Intel posted a third-quarter profit of $2.96 billion, or 52 cents a share, compared with $1.86 billion, or 33 cents a share, in the year-earlier period. Revenue was $11.1 billion, marking the first time the company's quarterly revenue has come in above $11 billion. In the year-earlier period, sales were $9.4 billion.\par \par \uc2 The energy, industrial and materials sectors fell as concerns were reignited over the potential impact of tightening measures from China. The country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  moved to temporarily raise its reserve requirements on six banks in an effort to manage liquidity and cope with inflationary pressure.\par \par \uc2 "The worry is that anything that China does might turn their boom into a bust," said Bill Stone, chief investment strategist at PNC Wealth Management.\par \par \uc2 Among stocks in focus, \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  edged up 10 cents, or 0.6%, to 17.48, after the drug giant said it will buy \uc2 King Pharmaceuticals\uc2  for $3.6 billion. \uc2 King Pharmaceuticals\uc2  soared $3.99, or 39%, to 14.14.\par \par \uc2 China's shares ended at a five-month high Tuesday, led by metals firms for the third consecutive session because of expectations the Federal Reserve will loosen monetary policy further and help drive up commodities prices.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the Shanghai index will likely test resistance at 2850 this week because of ample liquidity.\par \par \uc2 "Metals shares still have room to rise as investors expect prices for commodities to rise even further from their high levels currently," said Guan Yewen, an analyst at China Dragon Securities.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks fell sharply Tuesday as investors were spooked by the dollar's fall to near a 15-year low against the yen, while sentiment was also soured by heavyweight \uc2 Fast Retailing\uc2 's unexpected cut to its full-year profit forecast.\par \par \uc2 "There is going to be a tug of war (in the stock market) between the dollar's weakness (vs the yen) and wariness about further \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Mizuho Securities equity strategist Tsuyoshi Segawa.\par \par \uc2 Separately, Kenichi Hirano, operating officer at Tachibana Securities, said the Nikkei may once again fall below 9300 after the index breached key support at 9400.\par \par \uc2 Crude futures settled slightly lower in U.S. trading Tuesday as investors moved to lock in profits from the recent rally ahead of a meeting of oil ministers and crude inventory data due later this week.\par \par \uc2 Gold futures retreated Tuesday in the face of a strengthening U.S. dollar, ahead of the FOMC minutes, and as people took profits.\par \par \uc2 Base metals on the London Metal Exchange ended mostly higher Tuesday, bouncing back after the euro steadied against the dollar and U.S. equities pared early losses.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar lost ground Tuesday as the euro spiked above $1.39 after the minutes from the Federal Reserve's latest meeting indicated members of its policy-setting committee believe new steps could be needed to stimulate growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Fed's acknowledgement of the need for further support of the economy boosted demand for riskier assets, with the euro reversing earlier losses to hit a series of intraday peaks above $1.39. The yen, which had advanced earlier in the session despite the possibility of instigating further Japanese government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , extended its gains slightly after the minutes' release.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The [Federal Open Markets Committee] minutes reiterated much of the same thing, and overall indicated a very dovish tone," said Mary Nicola, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York. "The Fed do seem ready to pull the trigger on quantitative easing, if deemed necessary."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The minutes revealed "many participants" at the Sept. 21 meeting of FOMC believed "it would be appropriate to provide additional monetary policy accommodation" if the number of unemployed remained too high or prices too low.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar had fallen sharply in recent weeks as investors bet the next round of so-called quantitative easing by the Fed would involve major asset-purchase programs. But expectations for extreme steps had mitigated early this week, with the greenback rallying against the euro in response.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar fell against the yen despite warnings of possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from the Japanese government as investors viewed any action by Tokyo as not powerful enough to change market direction.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Tuesday afternoon trading in New York, the euro was at $1.3914 from $1.3877 late Monday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y81.74 from Y82.13, while the euro was at Y113.74 from Y113.97. The U.K. pound was at $1.5796 from $1.5873. The dollar was at CHF0.9570 from CHF0.9647.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at 77.339 from 77.500.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar had already given back some of its early gains prior to the publication of the FOMC minutes when a member of the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's governing council highlighted the contrast between the euro zone and the U.S.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Speaking in New York, Axel Weber, who also heads Germany's Bundesbank, warned that the risks associated with "exiting too late" from special stimulus measures are greater than possible negative implications from "exiting too early."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That indicates a further loosening of financing conditions in some of the world's largest economies won't, according to Weber, stand in the way of ECB's own plan to withdraw from unconventional measures to stimulate growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "These securities purchases should now be phased out permanently as part of our non-standard policy measures," Weber said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Separately, Japanese Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda reiterated Tuesday the government would take "decisive steps," including \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , if necessary, to stem the yen's rise.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I explained to the G-7 that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's previous \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was aimed at curbing excessive appreciation in the yen that could hurt the Japanese economy," Noda said at his first news conference since returning from a meeting of finance ministers and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials of the Group of Seven industrialized nations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Also, the dollar tested its all-time low against the Swiss franc at CHF0.9555 after the FOMC minutes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasurys were tripped up Tuesday by a lukewarm sale of $32 billion three-year notes and the minutes from the Federal Reserve's September monetary policy meeting.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bond prices also weakened as investors brace for a $21 billion 10-year note sale Wednesday and $13 billion 30-year bond auction Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At 1942 GMT, the benchmark 10-year note was down 4/32 to yield 2.411%, and the 30-year bond was down 25/32 to yield 3.791%. The three-year note was 3/32 lower to yield 0.556%. Bond prices move inversely to yields.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Most Fed policy makers believed additional monetary stimulus is needed to jumpstart the economy while warding off a deflation scare, the minutes showed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The focus of discussions centered on buying Treasury securities aimed to push down long-term borrowing costs for U.S. households, companies and the federal government. Fed officials also mulled options to influence inflation expectations as consumer prices have fallen below their desired target range of 1.5% to 2% in recent months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The minutes hurt Treasurys in two ways. The Fed's pledge to support the economy encouraged investors to buy riskier assets. U.S. stocks rebounded from early losses while the euro rose against the dollar. [ 12-10-10 2203GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the other hand, some Treasurys investors were disappointed the Fed didn't elaborate on the timing and the size of the Treasury buying, known as quantitative easing. That tempered bets that an announcement of bond purchases could come in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's November policy meeting.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Such wagers have fueled strong demand for Treasurys over the past few weeks, pushing yields on short-dated Treasurys to record lows. The two-year note's yield touched a record low of 0.331% earlier Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The tone of the minutes seemingly takes 'shock and awe' off the table in favor of a more prudent, data dependent expansion of the Fed's balance sheet," said Christian Cooper, head of U.S. dollar derivatives trading at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jefferies & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . in New York. "It possibly sets up the bond market for a disappointment."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The minutes, which were released one hour after the three-year notes auction, pushed some investors to step aside from the sale, traders said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Demand for the three-year notes was still healthy, as the securities were sold at a record-low yield of 0.569%. Yet the yield was above the 0.557% traded right before the sale, signaling a weaker-than-forecast bidding price for the supply.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's government bonds ended slightly lower Tuesday, reversing earlier gains as a sharp rise on the local equity market weighed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A Shenyin Wanguo Securities trader tipped the Shanghai exchange government bond index to consolidate in a narrow 126.5-126.8 range ahead of third-quarter economic data, including GDP, CPI and PPI, due next week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "PBOC Governor Zhou Xiaochuan's recent remarks suggest that Beijing has no plan to hike interest rates in the near term" and yields will likely stay in a range, the trader said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government bonds were higher Tuesday as the yen's appreciation further blurred the outlook for the country's export-reliant economy, prodding investors to continue buying safe-haven assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 JGB prices could extend gains as concerns grow that Japan's corporate sector will lose momentum and their performance will weaken, thus pressuring the Bank of Japan to take additional monetary easing steps to shore up the broader economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Long-term JGB yields "could still show some downside amid lingering expectations for further (BOJ) easing, including trends in (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign) exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2  chief strategist Chotaro Morita.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tetsuya Miura, chief market analyst at Mizuho Securities, said investors are paying close attention to Japan's machinery orders data, due out Wednesday, for clues as to whether companies have become reluctant to boost capital spending. Machinery orders are seen as a reliable gauge of future business investment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If the results turn out weaker than expected, that could damp hopes that capital spending will recover toward next spring. This would add to the view that deflation could deepen, with the yen's rise prompting firms to cut back investments in equipment, Miura said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Under such circumstances, demand for cash bonds won't likely wane soon, traders say.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Core machinery orders--which exclude often-volatile orders for ships and orders from electronic power companies--are expected to have fallen 4.5% on month in August following a 8.8% rise in July, according to economists surveyed by Dow Jones and the Nikkei.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among other data due Wednesday, China's trade surplus likely narrowed in September as import and export growth both slowed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The trade surplus likely fell to $18.5 billion in September from $20.03 billion in August, according to the median forecast in a Dow Jones Newswires poll of 13 economists. Exports in September likely rose 26% from a year earlier, slower than August's 34.4% rise, while imports likely rose 25%, down from the 35.2% growth rate in the previous month, according to the survey.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par Fed Officials Eye New Steps To Lift Growth \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Pfizer\f28 \uc2 \f2  To Buy King Pharma For $3.6B \par GMAC Mortgage To Review Foreclosures \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 JP Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2 , BofA, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wells Fargo\f28 \uc2 \f2  Tops In Foreclosures \par White House Ends Deepwater Drilling Ban \par ECB Weber Eyes End To Bond-buying Program \par Asian Nations Concerned Over Fixed-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Imbalances \par Canada Sees Return To Surplus In 2015/16 \par =WSJ: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alibaba Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  Creates New Search Website With \f28 \uc2 \f2 Microsoft\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par Intel Profit Up 59% On Robust Corporate Spending \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 2330  Australia    Oct \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac\f28 \uc2 \f2  - Melbourne Institute Consumer Sentiment \par                    Survey \par 2350  Japan        Aug Orders Received for Machinery \par 2350  Japan        Sep Bank Lending \par 2350  Japan        Sep Money Stock, Broadly-defined Liquidity \par 0030  Australia    Aug Lending Finance \par 0200  Japan        Sep Electric Power Output \par 0200  New Zealand  Sep NZ Government bonds held for non-residents \par 0530  France       Sep CPI \par 0800  France       Oct IEA Oil Market Report \par 0830  UK           Sep Unemployment \par 0900  EU           Aug Industrial Production \par 1100  US           Oct 8   MBA Mortgage Applications Survey \par 1230  US           Sep Import & Export Price Indexes \par 1740  UK           Bank of England MPC External Member Andrew Sentance speech \par 1800  US           Sep Treasury Statement of Receipts & Outlays of the U.S. Govt \par 2010  US           Fed Chmn Bernanke speaks on business innovation in \par              