\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: JGB Yields Could Fall Further - BarCap\b0\par\par\uc2 1,647 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2010\par 02:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0048 GMT [Dow Jones] Although JGB market has already digested BOJ's drastic easing announced last week, "yields could still show some downside amid lingering expectations for further easing, including trends in (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign) exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates," says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2  chief strategist Chotaro Morita; adds "with a 30-year auction set to take place on Thursday, the main focus (this week) will be whether the rapid steepening seen from the second half of last week shows further acceleration." 10-year cash JGB yield currently down 1.5 bps at 0.850%, while 20-year yield down 1.0 bp at 1.690%. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0044 GMT [Dow Jones]Kospi flat, last at 1889.92 as foreigners turn net sellers in early session. "There's some caution in the market ahead of Intel's third-quarter earnings and the FOMC meeting minutes (later in the global day)," says Shinhan Investment analyst Lee Sun-yup. But adds, major shifts unlikely as there's confluence of profit-taking and bargain hunting in market; tips 1894-1903 band for day. Automakers falling broadly amid worries that their profitability may be hurt by KRW's strength; \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) off 0.9% at KRW161,000, \uc2 Kia Motors\uc2  (000270.SE) 0.1% lower at KRW39,650. Banks broadly higher on 3Q earnings hopes; \uc2 Woori Finance\uc2  (053000.SE) +2.7% at KRW15,450, \uc2 Hana Financial\uc2  (086790.SE) +2.8% at KRW37,200. (vincent.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0040 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW breaches 1,120-level to trade higher at 1,126.5 vs 1,116.7 late yesterday in Seoul, tracking modest USD gains vs some majors overnight, with investors seen taking profits on riskier \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  (including KRW) after recent rallies, traders say. Local bank trader tips resistance in 1,127-1,128 range; says breach of 1,128 cap to 1,130-mark could trigger another bout of short covering on USD, giving pair another leg up, but that seems unlikely for now. "It's about time we saw some (upward) correction in the pair. But I don't believe the overall (downward) bias has been reversed yet," he says. Tips support at 1,120. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0038 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 0.1% at 9594.52 after opening 0.6% higher, then falling into negative territory. USD/JPY slightly weaker (pair now 82.28 after sliding on Monday to fresh 15-year low of 81.37) following Japanese Finance Minister Noda's reiteration government would take "decisive" action, including \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , if necessary. Index range for today pegged at 9500-9700, according to analysts. "It seems like Noda was able to obtain a certain level of understanding on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (at the G-7 meeting)," says Masayoshi Yano, senior market analyst at Meiwa Securities. "If the government does not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the event the dollar falls below 82 (again), the dollar may break below 80 yen." Projects Nikkei to rise above 9700 resistance if prospective \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  spurs USD/JPY rebound to 84-85 level. 18/33 Topix subindexes higher; \uc2 Central Japan Railway\uc2  (9022.TO) up 2.4% at Y642,000 on GS rating upgrade. \uc2 Fast Retailing\uc2  (9983.TO) off 5.3% to Y11,730 after cutting its FY profit forecast. \uc2 Nippon Paper Group\uc2  (3893.TO) down 1.6% at Y2,151 on Nikkei's grim FY1H pre-earnings report. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0034 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD likely to continue to slide; local trader tips pair to hit 30.600 early, with next near-term support at 30.300. Pair yesterday ends at 30.998 vs 31.095, after hitting new 2-year low of 30.645, as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected of buying about US$20 million to curb TWD rise, other traders say yesterday. "The trend of inflows is very firm and it doesn't look like the flows will abate in the near future." (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0034 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI may rise to fresh 33-month high, buoyed by upbeat investor sentiment ahead of unveiling of government's 2011 budget Friday, positive technical signals. KLCI ended +0.4% at 1487.41 yesterday, highest close since January 2008. "As is usually the case every year, market sentiment tends to turn more positive in the run-up to the budget as investors anticipate good news," dealer says, tipping KLCI to test 1500 psychological resistance near-term. In past 5 years, KLCI gained about 1%-2% during budget presentation week, analysts note. Plantation stocks such as \uc2 KL Kepong\uc2  (2445.KU) may rise after palm oil prices rallied to 26-month high. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0024 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR higher at 3.1030 vs 3.1000 late yesterday, following USD rebound vs other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; pair likely range-bound rest of the day in subdued trade as participants maintain cautious stance awaiting clearer outlook for global economy, release of 2011 government budget Friday, dealers say. Pair tipped to stay in 3.0900-3.1150 range near-term. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0010 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead December JGB futures open slightly higher on JPY uptrend, growing speculation over further easing in Japan, U.S.; contract currently +0.09 at 144.04. Until it becomes clear whether Fed will take additional easing steps at November meeting, USD/JPY's falling trend expected to continue and keep supporting JGBs, says Deutsche Securities strategist Makoto Yamashita. Adds although global economy slowing down, there're no fiscal, monetary policies which have immediate impact - lending downward pressure on JGB yields. 10-year cash bonds yet to change hands (yield at 0.855% at Friday's close). (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0004 GMT [Dow Jones] Flat session on Wall Street suggests Singapore stocks generally not expected to make much headway, although pockets of strength could still surface given positive newsflow from some companies. Resistance for STI, +0.3% at 3163.41 yesterday, expected at year-to-date 3197 high; support tipped at 3132 (Oct. 1 high). Keppel group may be in focus, with \uc2 Keppel Corp\uc2 . (BN4.SG) winning S$341 million waste-recycling contract, \uc2 Keppel Land\uc2  (K17.SG) reaping S$321 million gain from selling its one-third stake in Singapore's Marina Bay Financial Centre to \uc2 K-REIT Asia\uc2  (K71U.SG), which in turn will sell 2 office properties to developer for S$573 million. Keppel +2.3% at S$9.63 yesterday, \uc2 Keppel Land\uc2  +0.2% at S$4.11, K-REIT +1.5% at S$1.37. Asiasons WFG Securities says strong liquidity, expectations of more quantitative easing could continue to support shares, but tips effect as short term "as we experienced in the past year;" expects high risk of sharp correction as economic conditions, especially in U.S., not showing sustained improvement. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0002 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia government bonds likely rangebound as investors continue to bide time until key events later this week - auction of September 2017 MGS Thursday, release of government's 2011 budget Friday; until then, dealers say trading activity, which has slowed since last week, likely to remain subdued. Last week, goverment bond trading volume fell by 34% from previous week to MYR7.6 billion. "Expectations of sustained low interest rates are supportive for bonds, but on the other hand, players are of the view that there may not be much more upside room left following strong gains over the past few months," dealer says. Yesterday, yields on benchmark MGS end steady to a tad higher, with August 2015 +1 bp at 3.26%, September 2017 unchanged at 3.55%, November 2019 +1 bp at 3.66%. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0000 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY rises as investors cover short positions following comments by Finance Minister Noda, who says G-7 authorities in D.C. over weekend showed some understanding on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says Tomohiro Nishida, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 fx\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Chuo Mitsui Trust And Banking. Comments reinforce view, which grew after USD/JPY fell yesterday to fresh 15-year low at 81.37, that Tokyo may soon step into markets again to curb JPY strength. Nishida adds USD/JPY may rise to 83.00 by end of global day vs last 82.29. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2345 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 +0.3% with volume light as market looks for cues, says \uc2 Forsyth Barr\uc2  broker Dave Schaper; "We will likely follow Australia's lead. If they stay down, we will peg back slightly." Says \uc2 Goodman Property Trust\uc2  (GMT.NZ) +2.0% at NZ$1.01 after announcing, in exchange filing yesterday, \uc2 Downer EDI\uc2  had committed to purpose-built facility at its Glassworks development estate; "We all know Goodman is well run, we all know they've got development land and that they only build it when they have tenants sorted out so it's just a continuation of their business model and that could be viewed positively." Adds, all listed property companies higher as "the sector has been getting a good lift over the last few weeks;" Kiwi Income Property (KIP.NZ) +1.9% at NZ$1.07. Also adds, \uc2 Pike River Coal\uc2  (PRC.NZ) +1.7% at NZ$1.22 after saying yesterday it completed installation of its hydro monitor system; "It is also getting a lift off what has happened in Australia with resources (being strong) and investors are valuing up what Pike could be worth when it gets to full production." NZX-50 last at 3242.83. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 11, 2010 20:48 ET (00:48 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020101012e6ac0006t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
