\par\par \b \uc2 INDIAN MORNING BRIEFING: Sensex Could Test 20,270 Support\b0\par\par\uc2 1,695 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2010\par 05:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service\par \uc2 DJCS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GLOBAL MARKETS \par DJIA                11010.34  +3.86   +0.04% \par Nasdaq              2402.33   +0.42   +0.02% \par S&P 500             1165.32   +0.17   +0.01% \par FTSE 100            5672.40   +14.79  +0.26% \par Nikkei Stock        9504.39   -84.49  -0.88% \par Hang Seng           23132.93  -74.38  -0.32% \par Kospi               1869.71   -20.20  -1.07% \par SGX Nifty           6140.00   -15.00  -0.24% \par Oct. contract \par  \par USD/JPY             82.06-10     +0.13% \par Range               82.19-81.40 \par EUR/USD             1.3876-77    -0.84% \par Range               1.4011-1.3867 \par  \par CBOT Wheat Dec.     $7.092 per bushel \par Comex Gold          $1,354.40    +$9.10 \par Comex Gold Range    $1,341.10-$1,356.30 \par Nymex Crude (NY)    $82.21       -$0.45 \par  \par  \par CHARTING THE SENSEX \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bombay Stock Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2 's 30-share Sensitive Index could ease to 20,270, according to charts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Sensex continues to be rangebound and the market action is devoid of any meaningful trend. Though there is a possibility of a minor fall, the short-term trend remains bullish. The index has to close below major support at 19,860 to negate the bullish view.\par \par \uc2 While minor resistance is at 20,550, the Sensex has to move past major resistance at 20,670 to impart momentum to the short-term bullish trend.\par \par \uc2 The National Stock Exchange's 50-share Nifty could fall to 6,125.\par \par \uc2 But the short-term trend would remain bullish until the Nifty falls below major support at 6,050. While minor resistance for the index is at 6,150, it has to rise above major resistance at 6,190 to lend momentum to the uptrend.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par US STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks rose slightly Monday as investors grew cautiously hopeful for several bellwether earnings reports due in coming days.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The gains were limited as the dollar, which typically moves inversely to stocks, strengthened.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Monday's trading came on lighter-than-average volume, with just over 3 billion shares having changed hands in New York Stock Exchange Composite trading compared with the daily average of around 4 billion. Bond markets and federal offices were closed in the U.S. for Columbus Day holiday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With no major economic data or earnings reported Monday, investors were looking ahead to other key reports due this week, especially quarterly results due from bellwethers such as \f28 \uc2 \f2 Intel Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . and \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intel, which reports Tuesday afternoon, edged up 0.2% while the technology sector climbed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Apple Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . and \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Business Machines Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . hit notable highs. Apple finished up 0.4% at $295.36 after reaching an intraday record at $297.24. IBM gained 0.6% to $139.66 and earlier reached its highest intraday level in at least 39 years at $139.94.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  rose 1.1% ahead of its earnings report due Wednesday morning, but the broader financial sector fell.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ASIAN STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian share markets were mostly lower Tuesday after Wall Street's uninspiring lead Monday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan Nikkei Stock Average, Australia's S&P/ASX 200 and South Korea's Kospi Composite were all down while New Zealand's NZX-50 was up. Dow Jones Industrial Average futures were eight points lower in screen trade.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors in the Tokyo market were cautious after returning from Monday's holiday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fast Retailing\f28 \uc2 \f2  was down 5.3% after the company late Friday said its fiscal-year profit will decline for the first time in four years due to weak domestic sales.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Glassmakers were rising, with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Asahi Glass\f28 \uc2 \f2  up 2.0% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nippon Electric Glass\f28 \uc2 \f2  0.3% higher after \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  upgraded both companies' ratings to buy from neutral.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tech stocks were mixed with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sony\f28 \uc2 \f2  down 0.2%, Sharp 1.3% higher and Canon up 0.1%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Foreign-led selling weighed on the Seoul market, with auto makers leading early weakness.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Auto makers were down on worries that a strong won may hurt their earnings. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hyundai Motor\f28 \uc2 \f2  was off 0.9% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kia Motors\f28 \uc2 \f2  fell 0.2%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In contrast, banks were broadly higher on hopes of stronger third-quarter earnings. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Woori Finance\f28 \uc2 \f2  rose 2.7% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hana Financial\f28 \uc2 \f2  tacked on 2.8%.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro was at $1.3865 against the dollar, from $1.3877 late Monday in New York, and at Y114.07 against the yen, from Y113.97. The dollar was fetching Y82.23, compared with Y82.13.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par METALS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold futures ended higher Monday, recouping losses despite the strengthening U.S. dollar as investors continue to view any price pullbacks as bargain opportunities. Spot gold was at $1,352.40 per troy ounce, down $1.50 from Monday's New York close.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Base metals closed mixed on the London Metal Exchange Monday, with an announcement that ETF Securities will launch the first ever physically backed base metal exchange-traded fund doing little to move the markets. Some metals found support in inventory declines, but a stronger U.S. dollar weighed on the complex, market participants said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par OIL SUMMARY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude futures settled lower in U.S. trading Monday, though rising gasoline futures prevented a larger drop due to the strengthening dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 November Nymex crude oil futures were down 14 cents at $82.07 per barrel on Globex.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CALENDAR OF EVENTS Oct. 12 \par 1100 IST New Delhi:   August industrial output data \par 1130 IST Mumbai:      Skoda India director at launch of new Fabia \par 1130 IST Mumbai:      \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nokia India\f28 \uc2 \f2  managing director at press meeting \par 1145 IST Bangalore:   \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alcatel Lucent\f28 \uc2 \f2  president for services at press \par                       meeting \par 1200 IST New Delhi:   Bajaj Auto managing director at event \par 1230 IST New Delhi:   Finance minister at event \par 1400 IST New Delhi:   \f28 \uc2 \f2 Axis Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  chief executive, corporate banking \par                       executive director at launch Axis \par                       Infrastructure Index \par 1445 IST Mumbai:      \f28 \uc2 \f2 SKS Microfinance\f28 \uc2 \f2  chairman at press meeting \par 1630 IST New Delhi:   Federal cabinet meeting \par 0400 GMT EU:          September European Monster employment index \par 0830 GMT UK:          August trade \par 0830 GMT UK:          September inflation \par 1145 GMT US:          ICSC-\f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  Chain Store Sales Index \par 1400 GMT US:          September employment trends index \par 1620 GMT US:          European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  president speech \par 1800 GMT US:          Federal Open Market Committee meeting minutes \par 2100 GMT US:          Oct. 10 ABC News consumer comfort index \par 2301 GMT UK:          September Nationwide consumer confidence index \par  \p